{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bb5f530b60826c07", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tPages: 27\n2\t\n3\t===== PAGE 1 =====\n4\tBeyond Naturalness: Probing Automated Text-To-Speech Evaluators on\n5\tLinguistically Grounded Dimensions\n6\tOluwanifemi Bamgbose*, Simon Rosen, Jash Shah,\n7\tLindsay Devon Brin, Hoang H Nguyen, Anke Koelzer, Rachel Hansen,\n8\tTara Bogavelli, Fanny Riols\n9\tServiceNow\n10\tnifemi.bamgbose@servicenow.com\n11\tAbstract\n12\tAutomated Text-to-Speech (TTS) evaluation\n13\tmethods (Mean Opinion Score (MOS) predic-\n14\ttors and Audio Large Language Models (Audio-\n15\tLLMs) judges) are expected to reflect human\n16\tperception, yet it is unclear how well they cap-\n17\tture the distinct aspects of speech that listen-\n18\ters actually perceive.\n19\tWe deconstruct \"nat-\n20\turalness\" into a linguistically grounded an-\n21\tnotation schema spanning 10 distinct percep-\n22\ttual dimensions, and use it to construct the\n23\tfirst dimension-level meta-evaluation bench-\n24\tmark for TTS, comprising 860 utterances an-\n25\tnotated by trained linguist raters. Results from\n26\tbenchmarking four MOS predictors and four\n27\tAudio-LLMs judges reveal that MOS predic-\n28\ttors collapse onto acoustic signal quality, while\n29\tAudio-LLMs judges show selective, prompt-\n30\tdependent detection that does not generalise\n31\tacross all dimensions. Neither class reliably\n32\tcaptures a breadth of linguistically structured\n33\tspeech errors. Our dataset, annotation schema,\n34\tand evaluation code are publicly released to\n35\tsupport more targeted and interpretable TTS\n36\tevaluation.\n37\t1\n38\tIntroduction\n39\tText-to-speech (TTS) systems are increasingly de-\n40\tployed in production contexts where the natural-\n41\tness of synthesized speech directly affects user\n42\texperience and comprehension, with potential con-\n43\tsequences for user engagement (Tan et al., 2021;\n44\tXie et al., 2025). Listeners are sensitive to a mul-\n45\ttidimensional set of failures; a TTS system may\n46\tstress the wrong syllable in a word, break a sen-\n47\ttence at an unnatural point, or deliver a question\n48\twith the flat intonation of a statement, any of which\n49\twould be immediately human-perceptible (Geneva\n50\tet al., 2023; Gutierrez et al., 2021; Lee et al., 2026).\n51\tThe gold standard for measuring TTS quality\n52\tacross these dimensions remains human perceptual\n53\tjudgment (Baughan et al., 2023; Ulgen et al., 2026).\n54\t* Corresponding author.\n55\tTable 1:\n56\tCapability comparison with recent meta-\n57\tevaluation resources. ETE: EmergentTTS-Eval (Manku\n58\tet al., 2025); ITE: InstructTTSEval (Huang et al., 2025);\n59\tSJ: SpeechJudge (Zhang et al., 2025); SS: Speaker-\n60\tSleuth (Lee et al., 2026). ✓present, ~ partial, ✗absent;\n61\tpartial = human labels only validate a model judge\n62\t(ETE/ITE), or controlled difficulty without injected lin-\n63\tguistic errors (SS). Only ours pairs expert, aspect-level\n64\thuman labels with a linguistically-grounded taxonomy\n65\tand controlled error injection, auditing both MOS pre-\n66\tdictors and Audio-LLM judges.\n67\tCapability\n68\tOurs\n69\tETE\n70\tITE\n71\tSJ\n72\tSS\n73\tHuman-annotated ground truth\n74\t✓\n75\t~\n76\t~\n77\t✓\n78\t✓\n79\tDimension-level annotation per sample\n80\t✓\n81\t✗\n82\t✗\n83\t✗\n84\t✗\n85\tLinguistically-grounded aspect taxonomy\n86\t✓\n87\t✗\n88\t✗\n89\t✗\n90\t✗\n91\tCovers MOS predictors & Audio-LLM judges\n92\t✓\n93\t✗\n94\t✗\n95\t✓\n96\t✓\n97\tAs deployment scales, however, relying solely on\n98\thuman judgment becomes expensive and time con-\n99\tsuming. Two main paradigms have emerged to\n100\tautomatically evaluate speech quality at scale: neu-\n101\tral Mean Opinion Score (MOS) predictors (Mittag\n102\tet al., 2021; Saeki et al., 2022), trained to output in-\n103\tdividual, holistic quality scores that reflect human\n104\tMOS; and Audio Large Language Model (Audio-\n105\tLLM) Judges (Zhang et al., 2025; Manakul et al.,\n106\t2026), which prompt audio-capable LLMs to rate\n107\tor compare audio samples. A good speech eval-\n108\tuator should respond to all aspects of perceptual\n109\tquality to which humans attend, yet it is not clear\n110\twhether models of either type do so (i.e whether in-\n111\tdividual output scores align with quality along any\n112\tor all human-perceptible dimensions). For Audio-\n113\tLLMs in particular, it additionally remains an open\n114\tquestion whether they even have the underlying ca-\n115\tpability, and/or can be prompted, to attend to each\n116\tdimension.\n117\tTo better understand and identify the aspects of\n118\tspeech to which MOS models and Audio-LLMs ac-\n119\ttually attend, we construct the first meta-evaluation\n120\tbenchmark for automated TTS evaluators that\n121\thas annotations based on linguistically-grounded,\n122\thuman-perceptible speech dimensions.\n123\tOur contributions are as follows:\n124\tarXiv:2608.09930v1  [cs.SD]  10 Aug 2026\n125\t\n126\t\n127\t===== PAGE 2 =====\n128\tFigure 1: Dataset Construction Pipeline. Sentences from three sources (Harvard Sentences, EmergentTTS-Eval,\n129\tsynthetic text) are fed through three error-generation pathways: LLM-modified plain text, LLM-modified IPA\n130\ttranscriptions, and direct acoustic manipulation. All pathways pass through the TTS model to produce clean (Score\n131\t1) and error-targeted samples. Samples are then annotated by three linguists on all ten dimensions. After linguist\n132\tannotation, majority filtering and downsampling produce a balanced final dataset of 860 samples.\n133\t• Annotation Schema. A linguistically grounded\n134\tevaluation schema decomposing naturalness into\n135\t10 phonologically and phonetically grounded di-\n136\tmensions across word, prosodic, and paralinguis-\n137\ttic levels. This enables future evaluation to be\n138\taligned with human perception of speech quality.\n139\t• Meta-Evaluation Dataset.\n140\tA per-dimension\n141\tannotated dataset of 860 utterances, labeled by\n142\ttrained linguist raters across the 10 dimensions.\n143\t• Dimension-Level Audit. An evaluation of four\n144\tneural MOS models as well as four Audio-\n145\tLLMs judges across four prompting conditions,\n146\twith and without reference transcripts.\n147\tThis\n148\treveals uneven phonetic, prosodic and paralin-\n149\tguistic sensitivity, with neither MOS predictors\n150\tnor Audio-LLMs judges reliably detecting er-\n151\trors across these dimensions under any condition\n152\ttested.\n153\tOur benchmark enables researchers and practition-\n154\ters to attribute TTS system failures to specific in-\n155\tterpretable dimensions rather than opaque holistic\n156\tscores. This provides a diagnostic foundation that\n157\tprior evaluation frameworks have lacked.\n158\t2\n159\tRelated Works\n160\tAutomated TTS Evaluation.\n161\tThe dominant\n162\tparadigm for scalable TTS evaluation has been\n163\tMOS predictors: learning to approximate human\n164\tnaturalness scores from raw waveforms without a\n165\treference signal. Through the VoiceMOS Chal-\n166\tlenge series (2022–2024) (Huang et al., 2022,\n167\t2024), Semi-supervised Learning (SSL)-based\n168\tMOS predictors (Patton et al., 2016; Reddy et al.,\n169\t2021; Saeki et al., 2022; Baba et al., 2024) have\n170\tdemonstrated strong system-level correlation with\n171\thuman judgments, approaching the practical ceil-\n172\ting of aggregate naturalness scoring. However,\n173\tthese methods produce holistic quality signals\n174\twithout grounding them in independently inter-\n175\tpretable linguistic attributes, leaving no diagnostic\n176\tpath when a system performs poorly. More re-\n177\tcently, researchers have explored leveraging Audio-\n178\tLLMs as richer judges for TTS evaluation, evalu-\n179\tating speaking style (Chiang et al., 2025), speech\n180\tquality (Zhang et al., 2025; Manakul et al., 2026;\n181\tMonjur and Nirjon, 2025), and speaker consis-\n182\ttency (Lee et al., 2026). Yet, LLM-based judges\n183\tinherit the same limitation: scores remain holistic,\n184\tand the perceptual dimensions driving any given\n185\trating are opaque.\n186\tTTS Evaluation Benchmarks.\n187\tSeveral bench-\n188\tmarks have moved beyond scalar MOS towards\n189\tricher TTS evaluation (Manku et al., 2025; Huang\n190\tet al., 2025), yet share a fundamental limitation:\n191\tevaluation dimensions are organized around pre-\n192\tdefined scenario categories rather than a prin-\n193\tcipled speech quality schema.\n194\tEmergentTTS-\n195\tEval (Manku et al., 2025) introduces a model-\n196\tas-a-judge benchmark spanning six pre-defined\n197\tscenario types (emotions, paralinguistics, foreign\n198\twords, syntactic complexity, complex pronuncia-\n199\ttion, and questions) and demonstrates that Audio-\n200\tLLMs judges can distinguish broad scenario-level\n201\tvariation. InstructTTS-Eval (Huang et al., 2025)\n202\ttakes a complementary direction, centralizing eval-\n203\tuations on expressive and style-following capa-\n204\tbility of TTS systems across pre-defined dimen-\n205\tsions such as speaking rate, pitch, and emotional\n206\texpressiveness. However, neither provides human-\n207\tannotated ground truth at the attribute level, making\n208\tit impossible to audit whether automated evalua-\n209\t\n210\t\n211\t===== PAGE 3 =====\n212\ttors track the aspects listeners actually perceive.\n213\tWe bridge this gap with a human-annotated dataset\n214\tgrounded in 10 interpretable aspects across three\n215\tlinguistically-motivated quality dimensions, paired\n216\twith a calibrated annotation framework, enabling\n217\tdirect attribution of TTS system failures to specific\n218\tand interpretable aspects. More information related\n219\tto other benchmarks can be found at Table 1\n220\t3\n221\tBenchmark Design\n222\t3.1\n223\tEvaluation Schema\n224\tCurrent TTS evaluation conflates perceptually dis-\n225\ttinct failure types under a single quality judgment.\n226\tTerms such as “naturalness,” “fluency,” and “clarity”\n227\tare used interchangeably in MOS elicitation, yet\n228\tlisteners explain their ratings by invoking factors\n229\tranging from pronunciation accuracy to affective\n230\tdelivery (Kirkland et al., 2023), aspects that are\n231\tnot only perceptually distinct but defined at dif-\n232\tferent levels of linguistic structure. Drawing on\n233\tCrystal’s distinction between linguistic and paralin-\n234\tguistic features (Crystal, 1969), we organize evalu-\n235\tation around three levels corresponding to where\n236\teach property is linguistically specified: the lexi-\n237\tcon, the utterance, and the speaker. We propose a\n238\tten-dimension schema in which each attribute is\n239\toperationally defined and perceptually separable\n240\t(see Table 5).\n241\t3.1.1\n242\tWord level.\n243\tQuality at the word level is determined by whether\n244\teach word contains the correct sounds and carries\n245\tstress on the correct syllable. We organize this level\n246\taround fixed properties of lexical entries, including\n247\tboth phoneme identity and stress position. Errors at\n248\tthis level (whether phonetic or stress-related) con-\n249\tstitute word identity errors regardless of sentence\n250\tcontext (Jesse et al., 2017).\n251\tPhonetic accuracy: Whether the sounds in each\n252\tword fall within the acceptable range of realization\n253\tfor their lexical targets.\n254\tLexical stress: Whether primary stress falls on\n255\tthe correct syllable of each word, including appro-\n256\tpriate vowel reduction in unstressed syllables.\n257\t3.1.2\n258\tProsodic level.\n259\tQuality at the prosodic level is determined by\n260\twhether suprasegmental properties are appropri-\n261\tate for the syntactic and semantic properties of\n262\tthe utterance. Prosody is a perceptually separa-\n263\tble dimension that listeners isolate from overall\n264\tspeech quality (Gutierrez et al., 2021) and one\n265\twhere current systems remain measurably below\n266\tnatural speech (Vallés-Pérez et al., 2021).\n267\tWe define the dimensions below based on lin-\n268\tguistic function.\n269\tIntonation: The pitch contour over phrases\n270\tand sentences, encoding utterance type (e.g. ques-\n271\ttion, statement) and discourse structure (e.g. topic\n272\tboundaries, turn-taking signals).\n273\tProsodic stress: Prominence on specific words\n274\tor phrases relative to the rest of the utterance, de-\n275\ttermined by information structure.\n276\tProsodic boundary appropriateness:\n277\tThe\n278\tchunking of the utterance into prosodic phrases\n279\taligned with syntactic structure, realized through\n280\tboundary tones, lengthening, and pause.\n281\tSpeech rate appropriateness: The overall pace\n282\tof delivery, which can fail without any correspond-\n283\ting failure in pitch or prosodic structure.\n284\t3.1.3\n285\tParalinguistic level.\n286\tQuality at the paralinguistic level is determined by\n287\twhether the speech conveys speaker characteristics\n288\tappropriate to the intended speaker and content. Al-\n289\tthough paralinguistic dimensions share audio prop-\n290\terties with prosody, such as pitch and timing, they\n291\tconvey information about the speaker rather than\n292\tthe linguistic message. Following Crystal (1969),\n293\twe distinguish speaker-stable voice quality features\n294\tfrom affective features that modulate in response to\n295\tcontent and context (see Appendix 6.4). Where no\n296\texternal reference speaker exists, dimensions are\n297\tassessed relative to the system’s own output.\n298\tEmotional appropriateness: The degree to\n299\twhich the emotional tone matches the affective\n300\tcontent of the utterance.\n301\tExpressiveness: Natural variation in delivery\n302\tstyle and vocal energy, assessed relative to the sys-\n303\ttem itself.\n304\tHuman plausibility: The degree to which vocal\n305\tqualities fall within the range of physically realiz-\n306\table human production.\n307\tSpeaker identity consistency: Stability of per-\n308\tceived vocal characteristics across the utterance,\n309\tassessed relative to the system itself.\n310\t3.2\n311\tDataset Construction\n312\tOur dataset consists of 860 samples of annotated\n313\tand majority-voted audios. We generated synthetic\n314\tspeech using Cartesia Sonic-3 and introduced con-\n315\ttrolled errors through text modification and acous-\n316\ttic manipulation. Below we describe our source\n317\tcorpora, annotation scale, error generation meth-\n318\tods, and human annotation procedure.\n319\t\n320\t\n321\t===== PAGE 4 =====\n322\tTable 2: Error generation method, sample counts, and inter-annotator agreement per dimension. n is the balanced\n323\tevaluation set after majority-class downsampling to a 50/50 positive/negative split (positives = negatives = n/2 for\n324\tevery dimension). Krippendorff’s α is reported on this set with 95% bootstrap CIs. Total: n = 860.\n325\tLevel\n326\tDimension\n327\tMethod\n328\tText Source\n329\tManipulation\n330\tn\n331\tKrippendorff’s α\n332\tWord\n333\tPhonetic Accuracy\n334\tLLM-altered IPA\n335\tHarvard Sentences\n336\tPhoneme substitution\n337\t110\n338\t0.767 [0.660, 0.852]\n339\tLexical Stress\n340\tLLM-altered IPA\n341\tHarvard Sentences\n342\tStress mark transposition\n343\t90\n344\t0.821 [0.728, 0.907]\n345\tProsodic\n346\tIntonation\n347\tAcoustic manipulation\n348\tEmergentTTS-Eval\n349\tF0 contour manipulation\n350\t80\n351\t0.469 [0.332, 0.611]\n352\tProsodic Stress\n353\tLLM-altered plain text\n354\tHarvard Sentences\n355\tFunction word quoting\n356\t102\n357\t0.658 [0.542, 0.766]\n358\tProsodic Boundary Placement\n359\tLLM-altered plain text\n360\tSynthetically generated\n361\tStructurally targeted sentences\n362\t88\n363\t0.727 [0.604, 0.834]\n364\tSpeech Rate Appropriateness\n365\tAcoustic manipulation\n366\tHarvard Sentences\n367\tDuration scaling\n368\t128\n369\t0.707 [0.605, 0.801]\n370\tParalinguistic\n371\tEmotional Appropriateness\n372\tAPI-enforced emotion\n373\tSynthetically generated\n374\tText–emotion mismatch via Cartesia\n375\ttag\n376\t46\n377\t0.596 [0.400, 0.767]\n378\tExpressiveness\n379\tAcoustic manipulation\n380\tHarvard Sentences\n381\tF0 manipulation, loudness compres-\n382\tsion, vowel shortening\n383\t94\n384\t0.460 [0.332, 0.597]\n385\tSpeaker Identity Consistency\n386\tAcoustic manipulation\n387\tHarvard Sentences\n388\tCross-speaker fading\n389\t66\n390\t0.713 [0.577, 0.836]\n391\tHuman Plausibility\n392\tAcoustic manipulation\n393\tHarvard Sentences\n394\tInserted\n395\taudio\n396\tdistortions\n397\tand\n398\tglitches\n399\t56\n400\t0.710 [0.565, 0.849]\n401\tTotal\n402\t860\n403\tSource corpora.\n404\tTo systematically cover the\n405\tannotation schema dimensions, we selected ut-\n406\tterances from two existing corpora and synthet-\n407\tically generated additional texts where needed\n408\t(Table 2).\n409\tHarvard Sentences (IEEE Subcom-\n410\tmittee on Subjective Measurements, 1969) pro-\n411\tvide 720 phonetically balanced sentences cover-\n412\ting the English phoneme inventory broadly, well-\n413\tsuited to word-level and some prosodic dimensions.\n414\tEmergentTTS-Eval (Manku et al., 2025) provides\n415\tsentences with complex syntactic structures and\n416\tvaried question types (useful for prosodic bound-\n417\tary and intonation dimensions) alongside sentences\n418\twith explicitly defined affective content, used for\n419\temotional appropriateness. For dimensions lacking\n420\tappropriate utterances in these sources, we synthet-\n421\tically generated sample texts (Appendix 7.4).\n422\tScale design.\n423\tAll dimensions are rated as binary\n424\t(0/1), where 0 means any error is present and 1\n425\tmeans the audio is perfect, following the annotation\n426\tschema.\n427\tError generation pipeline.\n428\tFor each source sen-\n429\ttence, we generated utterances at the intended qual-\n430\tity level by introducing targeted errors via one of\n431\tthree pathways (see Figure 1):\n432\t• LLM-altered IPA. Used for Phonetic Accuracy\n433\tand Lexical Stress. An LLM (GPT-5) produces\n434\ta modified IPA transcription instantiating the in-\n435\ttended error, passed to Cartesia Sonic-3 (Carte-\n436\tsia AI, 2025) for IPA-driven synthesis (e.g.,\n437\tphoneme substitution: seed /sid/ →/sib/; stress\n438\ttransposition: harvest /\"hAr.vIst/ →/hAr.\"vIst/).\n439\t• LLM-altered plain text.\n440\tUsed for Prosodic\n441\tStress and Prosodic Boundary Placement. For\n442\tstress, quotation marks around LLM-selected\n443\tfunction words induce atypical prominence (e.g.,\n444\tType out three lists “of” orders); for bound-\n445\taries, commas split LLM-selected syntactic con-\n446\tstituents (e.g., I dropped my, keys).\n447\t• API-enforced emotion. Used for Emotional Ap-\n448\tpropriateness. Sentences with a predetermined\n449\temotion are synthesized via Cartesia with deliber-\n450\tately mismatched emotion tags (e.g., My beloved\n451\tdog passed away quietly in his sleep tonight. with\n452\ttag euphoric).\n453\t• Acoustic Manipulation. Used for Intonation,\n454\tSpeech Rate, Expressiveness, Speaker Identity\n455\tConsistency, and Human Plausibility.\n456\tPraat-\n457\tbased manipulation (Boersma, 2001) via Parsel-\n458\tmouth (Jadoul et al., 2018) introduces targeted\n459\tperturbations to a clean synthesis baseline: F0\n460\tcontour manipulation for intonation; duration\n461\tscaling for speech rate; combined F0, loudness,\n462\tand vowel duration modification for expressive-\n463\tness; cross-speaker fading for speaker identity;\n464\tand inserted distortions for human plausibility.\n465\tWe treat these as controlled upper-bound cases: a\n466\tjudge that cannot detect a deliberately introduced\n467\tperturbation cannot detect subtler naturally oc-\n468\tcurring deviations of the same type.\n469\tThe prompts and details of the acosutic modi-\n470\tfication are provided in Appendix 7.5 and Ap-\n471\tpendix 7.2\n472\t3.3\n473\tHuman Annotation\n474\tRaters.\n475\tEach sample was annotated by three pro-\n476\tfessional linguists with experience in analytical\n477\twork with natural language data. Three raters is the\n478\tpractical minimum for stable Krippendorff’s α esti-\n479\tmation; sample counts per dimension are reported\n480\tin Table 2.\n481\tPilot and guideline refinement.\n482\tTo reduce guide-\n483\tline ambiguity (Uma et al., 2022), we conducted a\n484\tpilot annotation (n = 50 samples) with all raters\n485\tand iteratively refined the guidelines after collec-\n486\t\n487\t\n488\t===== PAGE 5 =====\n489\ttive review of disagreements.\n490\tProcedure.\n491\tRaters are initially presented with the\n492\taudio only, but have the option to toggle the tran-\n493\tscript. Every sample is rated on all applicable di-\n494\tmensions, not only the dimension it was generated\n495\tto target. This yields cross-dimensional contam-\n496\tination data and allows us to quantify perceptual\n497\tspillover: when a sample was generated to elicit a\n498\tfailure on dimension X, what do raters observe on\n499\tdimension Y? (See Appendix 2)\n500\tInter-rater reliability.\n501\tTable 2 reports Krippen-\n502\tdorff’s α (Krippendorff, 2011) on the construct-\n503\taligned subset (n = 46-128 per dimension), after\n504\tclass balancing, with 95% bootstrap confidence in-\n505\ttervals (1117 resamples, percentile method, seed\n506\t42).\n507\t3.4\n508\tDataset Finalization\n509\tAggregation and downsampling.\n510\tGround truth\n511\tannotations for each sample, for each dimension,\n512\twere determined by majority vote. Each intended\n513\t(analysis) dimension was downsampled to a bal-\n514\tanced dataset of positive and negative samples, ran-\n515\tdomly sampling from the larger class with prior-\n516\tity for consensus annotations. Final dataset statis-\n517\ttics, including per-dimension sample counts, are\n518\treported in Table 2.\n519\t4\n520\tExperiments\n521\t4.1\n522\tJudges Evaluated\n523\tWe evaluate two classes of automated judge that\n524\tdiffer in output type and evaluation mechanism.\n525\tMOS predictors.\n526\tWe include UTMOSv2 (Baba\n527\tet al., 2024), DNSMOS-Pro (Cumlin et al., 2024),\n528\tNISQA\n529\t(Mittag et al., 2021), and Audiobox-\n530\tAesthetics (Tjandra et al., 2025). Each produces\n531\teither a single continuous scalar per audio or\n532\tdimension-wise scores.\n533\tAudio-LLMs judges.\n534\tWe evaluate a set of open-\n535\tsource and closed audio large language mod-\n536\tels: Gemini 3.5 Flash , Gemini 3 Flash, Qwen3-\n537\tOmni-30B-A3B and Step-Audio-R2-Mini, Audio-\n538\tLLMs judges are prompt-sensitive, motivating the\n539\tsystematic prompting conditions described in Sec-\n540\ttion 4.2.\n541\t4.2\n542\tPrompting Strategy Conditions\n543\tWe define four prompting conditions varying in\n544\tthe amount of schema guidance provided and the\n545\tgranularity of the expected output. In all conditions,\n546\tmodels produce binary labels matching the ground-\n547\ttruth annotation format. Each judge is queried three\n548\ttimes per sample per condition, with the majority\n549\tlabel taken as the final prediction. All conditions\n550\tare run with and without the reference transcript.\n551\tCondition 1: Underspecified MOS-style prompt.\n552\tThe model is prompted to rate the \"naturalness\" of\n553\tthe sample without a detailed schema, producing\n554\ta single holistic score analogous to a neural MOS\n555\tpredictor. This condition establishes what the judge\n556\tattends to in the absence of detailed task guidance,\n557\tand serves as the primary baseline.\n558\tCondition 2a: Schema-guided prompt, single\n559\tscore.\n560\tThe model is prompted with the full 10-\n561\tdimension schema but instructed to produce a sin-\n562\tgle overall score. Since this yields one score per\n563\tsample, we correlate it against human labels inde-\n564\tpendently for each dimension, treating the same\n565\tpredicted score as the candidate signal for each.\n566\tThis condition reveals whether schema exposure\n567\talone is sufficient to direct the judge toward the\n568\tright perceptual dimensions.\n569\tCondition 2b: Schema-guided prompt, per-di-\n570\tmension scores.\n571\tThe model is prompted with the\n572\tfull schema and instructed to produce a score for\n573\teach dimension, enabling direct per-dimension cor-\n574\trelation against ground-truth labels. This condition\n575\testablishes whether models can score all dimen-\n576\tsions simultaneously when given the a detailed\n577\tschema.\n578\tCondition 3: Isolated per-dimension prompts.\n579\tThe model is prompted for each dimension indi-\n580\tvidually. This condition reveals which dimensions\n581\tthe judge can reliably detect without interference\n582\tfrom the full schema. It tests dimension-specific\n583\tsensitivity in its purest form.\n584\tFull prompt text for all conditions is provided in\n585\tAppendix 6.7.\n586\t4.3\n587\tMetrics\n588\tFor each (rating dimension × model) pair, we eval-\n589\tuate how well model scores track binary human\n590\tground-truth labels.\n591\tPer-dimension effect size and significance.\n592\tKendall’s τb serves as a unified comparison metric\n593\tfor per-dimension effect sizes across both model\n594\ttypes: for continuous models (MOS predictors)\n595\tit measures rank-order correlation against binary\n596\tlabels, and for binary models (Audio-LLMs) it is\n597\talgebraically equivalent to Pearson’s ϕ. This equiv-\n598\talence enables consistent comparison of continu-\n599\tous and binary models within the same evaluation\n600\tframework.\n601\t\n602\t\n603\t===== PAGE 6 =====\n604\tTable 3: Per-dimension Kendall’s τ against human ground truth per evaluation dimension. AUDIO-LLMS judge\n605\trows use the C1 naturalness prompt (single holistic score, closest MOS analogue).\n606\tModel\n607\tWord\n608\tProsodic\n609\tParalinguistic\n610\tPhon.\n611\tLex.\n612\tInton.\n613\tPros.S\n614\tPros.B\n615\tRate\n616\tEmo.\n617\tExpr.\n618\tSpk.\n619\tHum.\n620\tMOS Models\n621\tAudioBoxCE\n622\t-0.051\n623\t0.161\n624\t0.371∗∗∗-0.241∗∗\n625\t-0.036\n626\t0.550∗∗∗\n627\t0.050\n628\t0.187∗\n629\t-0.270∗∗\n630\t0.606∗∗∗\n631\tAudioBoxCU\n632\t-0.052\n633\t0.015\n634\t0.098\n635\t0.068\n636\t0.048\n637\t0.485∗∗∗-0.025\n638\t0.418∗∗∗-0.177\n639\t0.511∗∗∗\n640\tAudioBoxPC\n641\t-0.058\n642\t-0.019\n643\t-0.099\n644\t0.337∗∗∗\n645\t0.152\n646\t0.020\n647\t-0.050\n648\t0.326∗∗∗-0.142\n649\t-0.615∗∗∗\n650\tAudioBoxPQ\n651\t-0.088\n652\t0.038\n653\t0.272∗∗\n654\t-0.169∗\n655\t-0.079\n656\t0.447∗∗∗\n657\t0.015\n658\t0.304∗∗∗-0.379∗∗∗\n659\t0.606∗∗∗\n660\tDNSMOS-ProBVCC -0.112\n661\t-0.055\n662\t0.092\n663\t0.121\n664\t0.179\n665\t-0.147∗\n666\t-0.061 -0.072\n667\t-0.196\n668\t0.450∗∗∗\n669\tDNSMOS-ProVCC\n670\t0.005\n671\t-0.121\n672\t0.026\n673\t-0.048\n674\t-0.038\n675\t0.071\n676\t0.128\n677\t0.406∗∗∗-0.092\n678\t0.397∗∗∗\n679\tNISQA\n680\t0.027\n681\t-0.047\n682\t0.068\n683\t0.142\n684\t0.099\n685\t0.236∗∗\n686\t-0.015\n687\t0.347∗∗∗-0.036\n688\t0.377∗∗∗\n689\tUTMOSv2\n690\t0.000\n691\t0.087\n692\t0.289∗∗\n693\t0.200∗\n694\t-0.024\n695\t0.390∗∗∗-0.062\n696\t0.099\n697\t0.050\n698\t0.313∗∗\n699\tAudioLLM Models\n700\tGemini 3 Flash†\n701\t0.323∗∗∗\n702\t0.251∗\n703\t0.125\n704\t0.125\n705\t-0.081\n706\t0.123\n707\t0.089\n708\t0.274∗∗\n709\t0.036\n710\t0.202\n711\tGemini 3 Flash△\n712\t-0.022\n713\t0.089\n714\t-0.225∗\n715\t0.099\n716\t-0.104\n717\t0.091\n718\t0.000 -0.050\n719\t0.036\n720\t0.121\n721\tGemini 3.5 Flash†\n722\t0.412∗∗∗\n723\t0.312∗∗\n724\t0.083\n725\t0.227∗\n726\t0.276∗\n727\t0.116\n728\t0.255\n729\t0.170\n730\t0.254∗\n731\t0.238\n732\tGemini 3.5 Flash△\n733\t-0.091\n734\t0.082\n735\t-0.026\n736\t0.111\n737\t0.230\n738\t0.000\n739\t0.045\n740\t0.135\n741\t0.068\n742\t0.219\n743\tQwen3 Omni†\n744\t0.000\n745\t-0.124\n746\t-0.113\n747\t0.039\n748\t0.064\n749\t0.214∗\n750\t0.105\n751\t0.061\n752\t0.293∗\n753\t0.000\n754\tQwen3 Omni△\n755\t0.173\n756\t0.192\n757\t0.026\n758\t-0.103\n759\t0.262\n760\t0.300∗∗∗\n761\t0.088\n762\t0.138\n763\t0.228\n764\t0.237\n765\tStep Audio 2 Mini†\n766\t-0.018\n767\t0.022\n768\t-0.128\n769\t-0.080\n770\t0.116\n771\t-0.096\n772\t-0.132\n773\t0.065\n774\t0.129\n775\t0.073\n776\tStep Audio 2 Mini△\n777\t0.019\n778\t0.023\n779\t0.100\n780\t0.040\n781\t-0.111\n782\t-0.078\n783\t-0.039 -0.021\n784\t0.095\n785\t0.036\n786\tModel Subscripts:: CE = Content Enjoyment; CU = Content Usefulness; PC = Production Complexity; PQ = Production Quality.\n787\tDNSMOS-Pro subscripts: BVCC = trained on BVCC corpus; VCC = trained on VCC2018 corpus. AUDIOLLM rows use the C1\n788\tnaturalness prompt. Columns: Phon. = Phonetic Accuracy; Lex. = Lexical Stress; Inton. = Intonation; Pros.S = Prosodic Stress;\n789\tPros.B = Prosodic Boundary; Rate = Speech Rate; Emo. = Emotional Appropriateness; Expr. = Expressiveness; Spk. = Speaker\n790\tIdentity; Hum. = Human Plausibility.ç Transcript: † = with transcript; △= without transcript. Bold = significant (p < .05).\n791\t∗p < .05, ∗∗p < .01, ∗∗∗p < .001 (two-sided).\n792\tTo test significance, for MOS predictors (contin-\n793\tuous output), we report Mann-Whitney U, which\n794\ttests for distributional separation between score\n795\tdistributions of positive and negative labels. For\n796\tAudio-LLMs(binary output), we also report Mc-\n797\tNemar’s test, which tests for marginal symmetry\n798\tbetween model predictions and ground truth.\n799\tFor MOS predictors, we additionally report AU-\n800\tROC, which is more naturally interpretable for con-\n801\ttinuous output and is equivalent to a normalized\n802\tMann-Whitney U statistic. AUROC rankings are\n803\tconsistent with effect sizes reported by Kendall’s\n804\tτb across predictors (Spearman’s ρ = 0.995).\n805\t4.4\n806\tMain Findings\n807\tMOS Predictors and AudioLLM Judges show\n808\tsensitivity to different dimensions.\n809\tUnder a ba-\n810\tsic naturalness prompt (C1), MOS predictors and\n811\tAudio-LLMs judges attend to fundamentally differ-\n812\tent aspects of speech quality. Neither class aligns\n813\treliably with human judgments across the full set\n814\tof perceptual dimensions. We observe that quality\n815\tdegradations in many dimensions go unnoticed by\n816\tthese models.\n817\tEvery MOS predictor reaches significance on\n818\tat least one dimension, with their attention con-\n819\tcentrating on paralinguistic dimensions generated\n820\tvia acoustic manipulation, while remaining insen-\n821\tsitive to word-level and prosodic dimensions (see\n822\tTable 3). Human Plausibility, constructed via in-\n823\tserted glitches, is the most consistently detected\n824\tdimension, reaching significance across all tested\n825\tmodels. Speech Rate Appropriateness, constructed\n826\tvia duration scaling, is also significant across 6 of\n827\tthe 8 models. Both patterns are consistent with\n828\tMOS predictors tracking signal-level degradation,\n829\treflecting their origins in telephony-era voice qual-\n830\tity assessment where such artifacts were the pri-\n831\tmary target. Expressiveness also reaches signifi-\n832\tcance for six of the eight models, though whether\n833\tthis reflects genuine sensitivity to expressive de-\n834\tlivery or a response to shared low-level acoustic\n835\tproperties across the Expressiveness, Speech Rate,\n836\tand Human Plausibility stimuli remains unclear.\n837\tIn contrast, no MOS models show significant de-\n838\ttection of Phonetic Accuracy, Lexical Stress, and\n839\tProsodic Boundary Placement, dimensions where\n840\tTTS systems produce linguistically meaningful er-\n841\trors that human listeners reliably detect, but MOS\n842\tpredictors do not.\n843\tSome significant correlations are negative.\n844\tWe\n845\tobserve a misalignment between some predictors\n846\tand human perceptual judgments. AudioBoxPC\n847\tshows a strong negative relationship with Hu-\n848\tman Plausibility; negative effects also appear\n849\tacross AudioBoxCE, AudioBoxPQ and DNS-\n850\tMOS ProBVCC on Speaker Identity Consistency,\n851\t\n852\t\n853\t===== PAGE 7 =====\n854\tTable 4: Per-dimension Kendall’s τ for AUDIO-LLMS judges using C2a, C2b, and C3 prompts (with and without\n855\ta provided schema, single or per-dimension outputs). Undefined τ (model predicted same value for all samples)\n856\tindicated by (.)\n857\tModel\n858\tWord\n859\tProsodic\n860\tParalinguistic\n861\tPhon.\n862\tLex.\n863\tInton. Pros.S\n864\tPros.B\n865\tRate\n866\tEmo.\n867\tExpr.\n868\tSpk.\n869\tHum.\n870\tC2a — Multi-dimension schema; single holistic score\n871\tGemini 3 Flash†\n872\t0.292∗∗\n873\t0.037\n874\t0.197\n875\t0.141\n876\t0.064\n877\t0.000\n878\t0.264\n879\t0.142\n880\t0.177\n881\t0.135\n882\tGemini 3 Flash△\n883\t0.206∗\n884\t0.327∗∗\n885\t-0.160\n886\t0.000\n887\t0.283∗\n888\t0.172\n889\t0.129\n890\t0.211∗\n891\t0.124\n892\t0.000\n893\tGemini 3.5 Flash†\n894\t0.486∗∗∗\n895\t0.294∗∗\n896\t0.119\n897\t0.216∗\n898\t0.064\n899\t0.000\n900\t0.396∗∗-0.031\n901\t0.122\n902\t0.146\n903\tGemini 3.5 Flash△\n904\t0.396∗∗∗\n905\t0.438∗∗∗-0.052\n906\t0.064\n907\t-0.104\n908\t0.102\n909\t0.099\n910\t0.170\n911\t0.296∗\n912\t0.115\n913\tQwen3 Omni†\n914\t0.097\n915\t0.146\n916\t0.052 -0.101\n917\t0.192\n918\t.\n919\t.\n920\t0.182\n921\t0.435∗∗∗\n922\t0.000\n923\tQwen3 Omni△\n924\t0.194∗\n925\t0.212∗\n926\t.\n927\t-0.141\n928\t0.000\n929\t.\n930\t.\n931\t.\n932\t-0.124\n933\t0.135\n934\tStep Audio 2 Mini†\n935\t0.000\n936\t0.186\n937\t.\n938\t-0.101\n939\t0.000\n940\t0.000\n941\t0.213\n942\t-0.147\n943\t0.413∗∗∗\n944\t0.135\n945\tStep Audio 2 Mini△\n946\t0.041\n947\t0.000\n948\t0.160 -0.039\n949\t-0.111\n950\t-0.221∗\n951\t0.000\n952\t-0.165\n953\t0.061\n954\t-0.153\n955\tC2b — Multi-dimension schema; one score per dimension\n956\tGemini 3 Flash†\n957\t0.397∗∗∗\n958\t0.106\n959\t.\n960\t.\n961\t0.196\n962\t.\n963\t0.149\n964\t0.104\n965\t.\n966\t.\n967\tGemini 3 Flash△\n968\t0.194∗\n969\t0.186\n970\t.\n971\t.\n972\t.\n973\t.\n974\t.\n975\t0.104\n976\t.\n977\t.\n978\tGemini 3.5 Flash†\n979\t0.514∗∗∗\n980\t0.290∗∗\n981\t.\n982\t.\n983\t0.137\n984\t0.126\n985\t0.309∗\n986\t0.211∗\n987\t0.177\n988\t0.135\n989\tGemini 3.5 Flash△\n990\t0.210∗\n991\t0.106\n992\t0.113 .\n993\t0.137\n994\t0.089\n995\t0.149\n996\t0.147\n997\t0.218\n998\t0.135\n999\tQwen3 Omni†\n1000\t.\n1001\t.\n1002\t.\n1003\t.\n1004\t.\n1005\t.\n1006\t.\n1007\t.\n1008\t.\n1009\t.\n1010\tQwen3 Omni△\n1011\t.\n1012\t.\n1013\t.\n1014\t.\n1015\t.\n1016\t.\n1017\t.\n1018\t-0.104\n1019\t.\n1020\t.\n1021\tStep Audio 2 Mini†\n1022\t.\n1023\t.\n1024\t.\n1025\t.\n1026\t.\n1027\t.\n1028\t.\n1029\t.\n1030\t.\n1031\t.\n1032\tStep Audio 2 Mini△.\n1033\t.\n1034\t0.095 .\n1035\t.\n1036\t.\n1037\t0.149\n1038\t.\n1039\t.\n1040\t.\n1041\tC3 — Single-dimension focus; one dimension at a time\n1042\tGemini 3 Flash†\n1043\t0.366∗∗∗\n1044\t0.000\n1045\t0.083 .\n1046\t0.319∗\n1047\t0.089\n1048\t0.213\n1049\t0.061\n1050\t.\n1051\t.\n1052\tGemini 3 Flash△\n1053\t0.261∗∗\n1054\t0.267∗\n1055\t0.000 .\n1056\t0.055\n1057\t0.021\n1058\t0.213\n1059\t-0.054\n1060\t.\n1061\t0.135\n1062\tGemini 3.5 Flash†\n1063\t0.355∗∗∗\n1064\t0.178\n1065\t.\n1066\t.\n1067\t0.243\n1068\t0.181∗\n1069\t0.459∗∗-0.060\n1070\t0.286∗\n1071\t0.135\n1072\tGemini 3.5 Flash△\n1073\t0.194∗\n1074\t0.170\n1075\t-0.155\n1076\t0.100\n1077\t0.276∗\n1078\t0.232∗∗\n1079\t0.303∗\n1080\t0.043\n1081\t0.218\n1082\t-0.135\n1083\tQwen3 Omni†\n1084\t.\n1085\t.\n1086\t.\n1087\t.\n1088\t0.137\n1089\t-0.184∗\n1090\t.\n1091\t0.026\n1092\t.\n1093\t0.165\n1094\tQwen3 Omni△\n1095\t0.136\n1096\t0.243∗\n1097\t.\n1098\t.\n1099\t0.298∗\n1100\t0.050\n1101\t-0.149\n1102\t-0.214∗.\n1103\t0.139\n1104\tStep Audio 2 Mini†\n1105\t.\n1106\t.\n1107\t.\n1108\t.\n1109\t.\n1110\t.\n1111\t.\n1112\t0.104\n1113\t.\n1114\t0.000\n1115\tStep Audio 2 Mini△.\n1116\t-0.108\n1117\t.\n1118\t0.100\n1119\t.\n1120\t.\n1121\t.\n1122\t0.069\n1123\t.\n1124\t0.038\n1125\tColumns: Phon. = Phonetic Accuracy; Lex. = Lexical Stress; Inton. = Intonation; Pros.S = Prosodic Stress; Pros.B = Prosodic\n1126\tBoundary;\n1127\tRate = Speech Rate;\n1128\tEmo. = Emotional Appropriateness;\n1129\tExpr. = Expressiveness;\n1130\tSpk. = Speaker Identity;\n1131\tHum. = Human Plausibility. Transcript: † = with transcript; △= without transcript. Bold = significant (p < .05). ∗p < .05,\n1132\t∗∗p < .01, ∗∗∗p < .001 (two-sided).\n1133\tProsodic Stress and Speech Rate. We hypothesize\n1134\tthat these predictors assign higher scores to the\n1135\tcontrolled acoustic distortions applied to the par-\n1136\talinguistic dimensions (see Appendix 7.3), rating\n1137\tdegraded samples more favorably than human lis-\n1138\tteners do.\n1139\tGeneric naturalness prompts yield sparse, in-\n1140\tconsistent sensitivity.\n1141\tIn contrast to MOS mod-\n1142\tels, Audio-LLMs judges prompted for a generic\n1143\tnaturalness rating (C1) show sparse and scattered\n1144\tattention to human-perceptible dimensions (see Ta-\n1145\tble 3). Gemini 3.5 Flash with transcript shows the\n1146\tbroadest coverage of any Audio-LLMs condition,\n1147\treaching significance on dimensions spanning the\n1148\tword-level, prosodic, and paralinguistic tiers, while\n1149\tevery other model and condition reaches at most\n1150\ttwo or three significant dimensions. Beyond the\n1151\tGemini family, sensitivity under C1 is sparse to\n1152\tabsent, with no dimension reliably detected across\n1153\tmore than one model, and the few significant ef-\n1154\tfects are directionally inconsistent. Taken together,\n1155\tthe C1 baseline shows selective sensitivity across\n1156\tthe Gemini models, while the other models show\n1157\tlittle evidence of reliable sensitivity to any dimen-\n1158\tsion under an underspecified naturalness prompt.\n1159\tSchema guidance helps selectively.\n1160\tWe find that\n1161\tschema guidance recovers word-level sensitivity\n1162\tabsent under the unguided naturalness prompt (C1):\n1163\tproviding the full 10-dimension schema while\n1164\tmaintaining a single aggregated score (C2a) in-\n1165\tcreases sensitivity to word-level dimensions for\n1166\tmodels that showed none under C1, suggesting that\n1167\tthis capacity was present but not elicited without\n1168\tschema guidance (see Table 4).\n1169\tThe clearest gain appears in the no-transcript\n1170\tcondition: both Gemini variants show no signifi-\n1171\t\n1172\t\n1173\t===== PAGE 8 =====\n1174\tcant word-level correlations under the basic natu-\n1175\tralness prompt (C1) but achieve statistically sig-\n1176\tnificant correlations when provided the detailed\n1177\tschema (C2a). This supports schema guidance\n1178\timproving model sensitivity on word-level dimen-\n1179\tsions, and underscores how under-specified a bare\n1180\tnaturalness prompt is for surfacing sensitivity to\n1181\twhich the model evidently has access.\n1182\tHowever, this benefit does not extend uniformly\n1183\tacross all dimensions: some correlations signifi-\n1184\tcant under C1 lose significance under C2a, while\n1185\tnew ones emerge. Paralinguistic dimensions re-\n1186\tmain largely resistant across all conditions, with\n1187\tsignificant correlations sparse and inconsistent.\n1188\tOutput collapse (where a model predicts the\n1189\tsame score for every sample, leaving correlation\n1190\tundefined) is also observed under C2a; for exam-\n1191\tple, Step Audio 2 Mini assigns the maximum score\n1192\tto every sample regardless of true label.\n1193\tWhen models are provided the schema and also\n1194\trequired to score each dimension independently\n1195\t(C2b), output collapse becomes the dominant fail-\n1196\ture mode. Two of the four models assign the maxi-\n1197\tmum score across nearly all dimensions and both\n1198\ttranscript conditions, leaving correlation undefined\n1199\tin almost all cells.\n1200\tTranscript access does not consistently increase\n1201\tsensitivity to any dimension.\n1202\tTranscript avail-\n1203\tability produces inconsistent effects across condi-\n1204\ttions and models, with no clean directional pattern.\n1205\tThe models that show sensitivity to word-level di-\n1206\tmensions under the unguided naturalness prompt\n1207\tC1 (Gemini 3 Flash and Gemini 3.5 Flash) only do\n1208\tso when the transcript is provided. However, once\n1209\tthe schema is provided (C2a), this dependency does\n1210\tnot persist; both Gemini variants become signifi-\n1211\tcant on the two word-level dimensions without the\n1212\ttranscript, although transcript provision is associ-\n1213\tated with stronger effect sizes.\n1214\tWhen models are prompted for each dimension\n1215\tindependently (C3), both Gemini variants remain\n1216\tsignificant on Phonetic Accuracy with stronger ef-\n1217\tfect sizes when the transcript is provided. For\n1218\tparalinguistic dimensions, Gemini 3.5 Flash also\n1219\treaches significance on Emotional Appropriateness\n1220\tin both transcript conditions. However, the pattern\n1221\truns the opposite way for paralinguistic dimensions\n1222\tfor two other models; Qwen3 Omni and Step Audio\n1223\t2 Mini show significant Speaker Identity sensitivity\n1224\tunder C2a only with the transcript, and show no\n1225\tsensitivity to the other paralinguistic dimensions in\n1226\teither condition.\n1227\tThe absence of a consistent transcript benefit\n1228\tsuggests that current Audio-LLMs judges are not\n1229\tconsistent in their ability to leverage textual ground-\n1230\ting to improve dimension-specific detection, even\n1231\tfor dimensions such as Phonetic Accuracy where\n1232\tthe reference transcript is directly informative.\n1233\tAudio-LLMs sensitivity remains inconsistent un-\n1234\tder per-dimension prompting.\n1235\tUnder C3 (per-\n1236\tdimension prompting), sensitivity remains sparse\n1237\tand inconsistent, with a pattern that largely dif-\n1238\tfers from C1 and C2a. An exception is Gemini’s\n1239\tincreased sensitivity to Phonetic Accuracy under\n1240\ttranscript provision, which holds across prompt\n1241\tvariations. Negative correlations still exist for some\n1242\tmodels on isolated dimensions. Step Audio 2 Mini\n1243\tshows no significant correlation under any tran-\n1244\tscript condition in C3, consistent with its perfor-\n1245\tmance across all other conditions and suggesting\n1246\ta fundamental sensitivity limitation rather than a\n1247\tprompting artifact.\n1248\t5\n1249\tConclusion\n1250\tWe\n1251\tpresent\n1252\tthe\n1253\tfirst\n1254\tdimension-level\n1255\tmeta-\n1256\tevaluation benchmark for TTS, decomposing natu-\n1257\tralness into 10 linguistically grounded perceptual\n1258\tattributes annotated by trained linguists. Across\n1259\tfour MOS predictors and four Audio-LLMs judges,\n1260\twe find that current automated evaluators ex-\n1261\thibit systematic but distinct blind spots: MOS\n1262\tpredictors align strongly with signal-level arti-\n1263\tfacts but fail on word-level and prosodic dimen-\n1264\tsions, whereas Audio-LLMs judges show selective,\n1265\tprompt-dependent detection that never generalises\n1266\tacross the full dimension set.\n1267\tWe further show that how Audio-LLMs judges\n1268\tare prompted in practice substantially affects their\n1269\tmeasured performance: querying dimensions in\n1270\tisolation consistently improves alignment with hu-\n1271\tman judgments, while asking models to score\n1272\tall dimensions jointly degrades output reliability\n1273\tthrough prediction collapse.\n1274\tTaken together, our findings suggest that natural-\n1275\tness cannot be treated as a single scalar construct\n1276\tfor modern TTS evaluation and that robust evalua-\n1277\ttion will require dimension-aware benchmarks.\n1278\tLimitations\n1279\tEcological validity. Due to the difficulty of sourc-\n1280\ting naturally occurring TTS failures at scale across\n1281\tspecific perceptual dimensions, errors were elicited\n1282\tthrough TTS prompting and Praat manipulation.\n1283\t\n1284\t\n1285\t===== PAGE 9 =====\n1286\tPraat-manipulated stimuli are thus best understood\n1287\tas controlled upper-bound conditions rather than\n1288\trepresentative deployment samples.\n1289\tStimulus complexity ceiling. Harvard Sentences\n1290\taverage 7–8 words with SVO structure, limited dis-\n1291\tcourse context, and few idioms or proper nouns.\n1292\tThis is ideal for eliciting clear word-level failures,\n1293\tbut limits generalisability to more complex natural-\n1294\tistic speech where prosodic and syntactic interac-\n1295\ttions are richer.\n1296\tSingle architecture for IPA-controlled genera-\n1297\ttion. Phoneme-level input is currently only sup-\n1298\tported by Cartesia Sonic among the evaluated\n1299\tproviders; all IPA-driven samples consequently\n1300\treflect one TTS architecture. We identify multi-\n1301\tsystem evaluation as a priority for future work.\n1302\tAnnotation scale granularity. Binary scales\n1303\tdo not capture full fine-grained quality gradients.\n1304\tIn contrast, ordinal scales provide granularity but\n1305\talso the potential for response biases and incon-\n1306\tsistent boundary interpretation between levels. In\n1307\tthis context, binary scales were a deliberate scope\n1308\tchoice; the benchmark targets error detection, not\n1309\tfull perceptual grading.\n1310\tAnnotation reliability. Majority-class downsam-\n1311\tpling to achieve class balance reduces effective\n1312\tsample sizes, most consequentially for Emotional\n1313\tAppropriateness (n=46) and Lexical Stress (n=90),\n1314\twhere agreement estimates carry wider confidence\n1315\tintervals.\n1316\tEthics Statement\n1317\tOur proposed benchmark is intended solely to sup-\n1318\tport more targeted and interpretable TTS evalua-\n1319\ttion research and is not intended for discrimina-\n1320\ttory applications or commercial ranking of TTS\n1321\tproviders. All annotators are first-language English\n1322\tspeakers and trained linguists who participated vol-\n1323\tuntarily at standard rate compensation. No sensi-\n1324\ttive or personally identifiable data was collected.\n1325\tAll of our speech samples are synthetically gen-\n1326\terated or acoustically manipulated; no real human\n1327\tspeech recordings were collected. Source corpora\n1328\tand APIs are used in accordance with their re-\n1329\tspective licenses and terms of service (see Ap-\n1330\tpendix 7.5).\n1331\tRegarding Large Language Model (LLM) usage\n1332\tin manuscript preparation, we utilize them solely to\n1333\trefine the language used in paper to improve clarity\n1334\tand correctness, without generating any substantial\n1335\tcontent or claims.\n1336\tReferences\n1337\tKaito Baba, Wataru Nakata, Yuki Saito, and Hiroshi\n1338\tSaruwatari. 2024. The t05 system for the VoiceMOS\n1339\tChallenge 2024: Transfer learning from deep im-\n1340\tage classifier to naturalness MOS prediction of high-\n1341\tquality synthetic speech. In IEEE Spoken Language\n1342\tTechnology Workshop (SLT).\n1343\tAmanda Baughan, Xuezhi Wang, Ariel Liu, Allison\n1344\tMercurio, Jilin Chen, and Xiao Ma. 2023. A mixed-\n1345\tmethods approach to understanding user trust after\n1346\tvoice assistant failures. In Proceedings of the 2023\n1347\tCHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing\n1348\tSystems, pages 1–16.\n1349\tPaul Boersma. 2001. Praat, a system for doing pho-\n1350\tnetics by computer. Glot International, 5(9/10):341–\n1351\t345.\n1352\tCartesia AI. 2025.\n1353\tSonic-3:\n1354\tStreaming text-\n1355\tto-speech model.\n1356\thttps://docs.cartesia.ai/\n1357\tbuild-with-cartesia/tts-models/latest. Ac-\n1358\tcessed: 2025.\n1359\tCheng-Han Chiang, Xiaofei Wang, Chung-Ching Lin,\n1360\tKevin Lin, Linjie Li, Radu Kopetz, Yao Qian, Zhen-\n1361\tdong Wang, Zhengyuan Yang, Hung-yi Lee, and Li-\n1362\tjuan Wang. 2025. Audio-aware large language mod-\n1363\tels as judges for speaking styles. In Findings of the\n1364\tAssociation for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP\n1365\t2025, pages 467–480, Suzhou, China. Association\n1366\tfor Computational Linguistics.\n1367\tDavid Crystal. 1969. Prosodic Systems and Intonation\n1368\tin English. Cambridge University Press, Cambridge.\n1369\tFredrik Cumlin, Xinyu Liang, Victor Ungureanu, Chan-\n1370\tdan K. A. Reddy, Christian Schüldt, and Saikat Chat-\n1371\tterjee. 2024. DNSMOS Pro: A Reduced-Size DNN\n1372\tfor Probabilistic MOS of Speech. In Interspeech\n1373\t2024, pages 4818–4822.\n1374\tDiana Geneva, Georgi Shopov, Kostadin Garov, Maria\n1375\tTodorova, Stefan Gerdjikov, and Stoyan Mihov. 2023.\n1376\tAccentor: An Explicit Lexical Stress Model for TTS\n1377\tSystems. In Interspeech 2023, pages 4848–4852.\n1378\tElijah Gutierrez, Pilar Oplustil-Gallegos, and Cather-\n1379\tine Lai. 2021. Location, location: Enhancing the\n1380\tevaluation of text-to-speech synthesis using the rapid\n1381\tprosody transcription paradigm. In Proc. SSW 2021,\n1382\tpages 25–30.\n1383\tKexin Huang, Qian Tu, Liwei Fan, Chenchen Yang,\n1384\tDong Zhang, Shimin Li, Zhaoye Fei, Qinyuan Cheng,\n1385\tand Xipeng Qiu. 2025.\n1386\tInstructttseval:\n1387\tBench-\n1388\tmarking complex natural-language instruction fol-\n1389\tlowing in text-to-speech systems.\n1390\tarXiv preprint\n1391\tarXiv:2506.16381.\n1392\tWen Chin Huang, Erica Cooper, Yu Tsao, Hsin-Min\n1393\tWang, Tomoki Toda, and Junichi Yamagishi. 2022.\n1394\tThe voicemos challenge 2022. In Proc. Interspeech\n1395\t2022, pages 4536–4540.\n1396\t\n1397\t\n1398\t===== PAGE 10 =====\n1399\tWen-Chin Huang, Szu-Wei Fu, Erica Cooper, Ryand-\n1400\thimas E Zezario, Tomoki Toda, Hsin-Min Wang, Ju-\n1401\tnichi Yamagishi, and Yu Tsao. 2024. The voicemos\n1402\tchallenge 2024: Beyond speech quality prediction.\n1403\tIn 2024 IEEE Spoken Language Technology Work-\n1404\tshop (SLT), pages 803–810. IEEE.\n1405\tIEEE Subcommittee on Subjective Measurements. 1969.\n1406\tIEEE recommended practice for speech quality mea-\n1407\tsurements. IEEE Std 297-1969. Appendix C: 1965\n1408\tRevised List of Phonetically Balanced Sentences\n1409\t(Harvard Sentences).\n1410\tYannick Jadoul, Bill Thompson, and Bart de Boer. 2018.\n1411\tIntroducing Parselmouth: A Python interface to Praat.\n1412\tJournal of Phonetics, 71:1–15.\n1413\tAlexandra Jesse, Katja Poellmann, and Ying-Yee Kong.\n1414\t2017. English listeners use suprasegmental cues to\n1415\tlexical stress early during spoken-word recognition.\n1416\tJournal of Speech, Language, and Hearing Research,\n1417\t60(1):190–198.\n1418\tAmbika Kirkland, Shivam Mehta, Harm Lameris, Gus-\n1419\ttav Eje Henter, Eva Szekely, and Joakim Gustafson.\n1420\t2023. Stuck in the MOS pit: A critical analysis of\n1421\tMOS test methodology in TTS evaluation. In 12th\n1422\tISCA Speech Synthesis Workshop (SSW2023), pages\n1423\t41–47.\n1424\tKlaus Krippendorff. 2011. Computing Krippendorff’s\n1425\talpha-reliability. Departmental papers, University\n1426\tof Pennsylvania, Annenberg School for Communica-\n1427\ttion, Philadelphia, PA.\n1428\tJonggeun Lee, Junseong Pyo, Gyuhyeon Seo, and\n1429\tYohan\n1430\tJo.\n1431\t2026.\n1432\tSpeakersleuth:\n1433\tEvaluat-\n1434\ting large audio-language models as judges for\n1435\tmulti-turn speaker consistency.\n1436\tarXiv preprint\n1437\tarXiv:2601.04029.\n1438\tPotsawee Manakul, Woody Haosheng Gan, Michael J\n1439\tRyan, Ali Sartaz Khan, Warit Sirichotedumrong, Ku-\n1440\tnat Pipatanakul, William Barr Held, and Diyi Yang.\n1441\t2026. Audiojudge: Understanding what works in\n1442\tlarge audio model based speech evaluation. In Pro-\n1443\tceedings of the 19th Conference of the European\n1444\tChapter of the Association for Computational Lin-\n1445\tguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), pages 3644–3663.\n1446\tRuskin Raj Manku, Yuzhi Tang, Xingjian Shi, Mu Li,\n1447\tand Alexander Smola. 2025. Emergenttts-eval: Eval-\n1448\tuating tts models on complex prosodic, expressive-\n1449\tness, and linguistic challenges using model-as-a-\n1450\tjudge. 38.\n1451\tGabriel Mittag, Babak Naderi, Assmaa Chehadi, and\n1452\tSebastian Möller. 2021. Nisqa: A deep cnn-self-\n1453\tattention model for multidimensional speech qual-\n1454\tity prediction with crowdsourced datasets. arXiv\n1455\tpreprint arXiv:2104.09494.\n1456\tMahathir\n1457\tMonjur\n1458\tand\n1459\tShahriar\n1460\tNirjon.\n1461\t2025.\n1462\tSpeechqualityllm:\n1463\tLlm-based\n1464\tmultimodal\n1465\tas-\n1466\tsessment of speech quality.\n1467\tarXiv preprint\n1468\tarXiv:2512.08238.\n1469\tBrian Patton, Yannis Agiomyrgiannakis, Michael Terry,\n1470\tKevin Wilson, Rif A. Saurous, and D. Sculley.\n1471\t2016. Automos: Learning a non-intrusive assessor\n1472\tof naturalness-of-speech. In NIPS 2016 End-to-end\n1473\tLearning for Speech and Audio Processing Work-\n1474\tshop.\n1475\tChandan KA Reddy, Vishak Gopal, and Ross Cutler.\n1476\t2021. Dnsmos: A non-intrusive perceptual objective\n1477\tspeech quality metric to evaluate noise suppressors.\n1478\tIn ICASSP 2021-2021 IEEE International Confer-\n1479\tence on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n1480\t(ICASSP), pages 6493–6497. IEEE.\n1481\tTakaaki Saeki, Detai Xin, Wataru Nakata, Tomoki\n1482\tKoriyama, Shinnosuke Takamichi, and Hiroshi\n1483\tSaruwatari. 2022. Utmos: Utokyo-sarulab system\n1484\tfor voicemos challenge 2022. In Proc. Interspeech\n1485\t2022, pages 4521–4525.\n1486\tXu Tan, Tao Qin, Frank Soong, and Tie-Yan Liu. 2021.\n1487\tA survey on neural speech synthesis. arXiv preprint\n1488\tarXiv:2106.15561.\n1489\tAndros Tjandra, Yi-Chiao Wu, Baishan Guo, John Hoff-\n1490\tman, Brian Ellis, Apoorv Vyas, Bowen Shi, Sanyuan\n1491\tChen, Matt Le, Nick Zacharov, and 1 others. 2025.\n1492\tMeta audiobox aesthetics: Unified automatic qual-\n1493\tity assessment for speech, music, and sound. arXiv\n1494\tpreprint arXiv:2502.05139.\n1495\tIsmail Rasim Ulgen, Zongyang Du, Junchen Lu, Philipp\n1496\tKoehn, and Berrak Sisman. 2026. Objective evalua-\n1497\ttion of prosody and intelligibility in speech synthesis\n1498\tvia conditional prediction of discrete tokens. IEEE\n1499\tOpen Journal of Signal Processing.\n1500\tAlexandra N. Uma, Tommaso Fornaciari, Dirk Hovy,\n1501\tSilviu Paun, Barbara Plank, and Massimo Poesio.\n1502\t2022. Learning from disagreement: A survey. J.\n1503\tArtif. Int. Res., 72:1385–1470.\n1504\tIván Vallés-Pérez, Julian Roth, Grzegorz Beringer,\n1505\tRoberto Barra-Chicote, and Jasha Droppo. 2021. Im-\n1506\tproving Multi-Speaker TTS Prosody Variance with a\n1507\tResidual Encoder and Normalizing Flows. In Inter-\n1508\tspeech 2021, pages 3131–3135.\n1509\tTianxin Xie, Yan Rong, Pengfei Zhang, Wenwu Wang,\n1510\tand Li Liu. 2025. Towards controllable speech syn-\n1511\tthesis in the era of large language models: A system-\n1512\tatic survey. In Proceedings of the 2025 Conference\n1513\ton Empirical Methods in Natural Language Process-\n1514\ting, pages 764–791.\n1515\tXueyao Zhang, Chaoren Wang, Huan Liao, Ziniu\n1516\tLi, Yuancheng Wang, Li Wang, Dongya Jia,\n1517\tYuanzhe Chen, Xiulin Li, Zhuo Chen, and 1 oth-\n1518\ters. 2025.\n1519\tSpeechjudge:\n1520\tTowards human-level\n1521\tjudgment for speech naturalness.\n1522\tarXiv preprint\n1523\tarXiv:2511.07931.\n1524\t\n1525\t\n1526\t===== PAGE 11 =====\n1527\t6\n1528\tAppendix\n1529\tGlossary of Terms\n1530\tLinguistic Theory\n1531\tAcoustic.Of or relating to the physical properties\n1532\tof sound as a mechanical wave. In speech sci-\n1533\tence, acoustic analysis involves measurements of\n1534\tfrequency, amplitude, duration, and spectral char-\n1535\tacteristics derived from the audio signal.\n1536\tLexical.Of or relating to the words or vocabulary\n1537\tof a language, as distinct from its grammatical\n1538\tstructure.\n1539\tIn speech, lexical properties include\n1540\tword identity, lexical stress assignment, and phono-\n1541\tlogical form.\n1542\tLinguistic.Of or relating to language or linguistics.\n1543\tIn speech evaluation, pertaining to features con-\n1544\tventionally encoded in the grammar of a language,\n1545\tincluding segmental and prosodic structure.\n1546\tPhoneme.The smallest contrastive unit of sound\n1547\tcapable of distinguishing meaning. Phonemes are\n1548\tabstract categories realized by phonetically simi-\n1549\tlar variants (allophones) whose distribution is gov-\n1550\terned by phonological context.\n1551\tPhonetics.The branch of linguistics concerned\n1552\twith the physical and perceptual properties of\n1553\tspeech sounds.\n1554\tPhonology.The branch of linguistics concerned\n1555\twith the abstract, rule-governed organization of\n1556\tsound systems. Phonology describes the system-\n1557\tatic patterns governing the distribution and combi-\n1558\tnation of phonemes.\n1559\tSegmental.Pertaining to individual consonant and\n1560\tvowel segments comprising the phonemic inven-\n1561\ttory of a language. Segmental quality encompasses\n1562\tplace and manner of articulation, voicing, vowel\n1563\tquality, and allophonic processes such as aspiration\n1564\tand flapping.\n1565\tSemantic.Of or relating to meaning in language.\n1566\tIn TTS evaluation, semantic considerations include\n1567\twhether prosodic realization—particularly intona-\n1568\ttion and focus—accurately reflects information\n1569\tstructure, such as given/new status and contrastive\n1570\temphasis.\n1571\tSuprasegmental.Pertaining to phonological fea-\n1572\ttures spanning units larger than a single segment:\n1573\tstress, pitch accent, intonation, rhythm, and bound-\n1574\tary placement. Also termed prosodic features.\n1575\tSyntactic.Of or relating to the grammatical struc-\n1576\tture and word order of sentences. Syntactic con-\n1577\tstituent boundaries frequently align with prosodic\n1578\tboundaries, and syntactic relationships influence\n1579\tprominence placement and intonational contour\n1580\tshape.\n1581\tParalinguistic.Pertaining to communicative cues\n1582\tthat accompany linguistic expression without con-\n1583\tstituting part of its lexical, grammatical, or phono-\n1584\tlogical structure. Such cues may convey affect,\n1585\tstance, or speaker identity. Although paralinguistic\n1586\tfeatures may be vocal, visual, gestural, or other-\n1587\twise embodied, this glossary uses the term in the\n1588\tnarrower context of TTS evaluation to refer only to\n1589\tvocal cues.\n1590\tNotational\n1591\tf0 (Fundamental Frequency) The lowest fre-\n1592\tquency component of a voiced speech waveform,\n1593\tcorresponding to the rate of vocal fold vibration. f0\n1594\tis the primary acoustic correlate of perceived pitch\n1595\tand a key parameter of intonation and stress.\n1596\tIPA (International Phonetic Alphabet) A stan-\n1597\tdardized phonetic notation system maintained by\n1598\tthe International Phonetic Association, providing a\n1599\tunique symbol for every sound in any human lan-\n1600\tguage. Used for both broad (phonemic) and narrow\n1601\t(phonetic) transcription.\n1602\tSpeech & Language Technology\n1603\tLALM (Large Audio Language Model) A mul-\n1604\ttimodal extension of large language models incor-\n1605\tporating audio as an input and/or output modality.\n1606\tLALMs can process and generate spoken language,\n1607\tin many architectures subsuming TTS and ASR\n1608\twithin a unified model.\n1609\tLLM (Large Language Model) A neural lan-\n1610\tguage model trained on large-scale text corpora\n1611\tvia self-supervised objectives, capable of genera-\n1612\ttion, classification, and reasoning across a wide\n1613\trange of text-based tasks.\n1614\tTTS (Text-to-Speech) A class of speech synthesis\n1615\ttechnology converting written text to spoken audio.\n1616\tModern neural TTS systems leverage sequence-to-\n1617\tsequence architectures and neural vocoders trained\n1618\tat scale to produce high-fidelity synthetic speech.\n1619\tMOS (Mean Opinion Score) A perceptual evalu-\n1620\tation metric in which listeners rate stimulus quality\n1621\ton a scale of 1–5; the MOS is the arithmetic mean\n1622\tof collected ratings. Originally standardized for\n1623\ttelephony (ITU-T P.800), MOS and its variants are\n1624\tstandard in TTS evaluation.\n1625\t\n1626\t\n1627\t===== PAGE 12 =====\n1628\tTable 5: The ten-aspect evaluation schema organized by dimension. Full annotation criteria and worked examples\n1629\tare provided in Table 6.\n1630\tLevel\n1631\tDimension\n1632\tFailure condition\n1633\tWord\n1634\tPhonetic accuracy\n1635\tSound outside acceptable range of lexical target\n1636\tLexical stress\n1637\tStress assigned to wrong syllable\n1638\tProsodic\n1639\tIntonation\n1640\tPitch contour inappropriate to utterance type or pragmatic mean-\n1641\ting\n1642\tProsodic stress\n1643\tPost-lexical prominence misplaced relative to information struc-\n1644\tture\n1645\tProsodic boundary placement\n1646\tPhrase boundaries inconsistent with syntactic structure\n1647\tSpeech rate appropriateness\n1648\tTempo inappropriate to context\n1649\tParalinguistic\n1650\tExpressiveness\n1651\tAffective range insufficient or excessive for content\n1652\tEmotional appropriateness\n1653\tEmotional tone inconsistent with content\n1654\tSpeaker identity consistency\n1655\tVoice characteristics vary within the utterance\n1656\tHuman plausibility\n1657\tVocal qualities outside physically realizable range\n1658\t6.1\n1659\tData Scale\n1660\t6.1.1\n1661\tScale Collapse\n1662\tFor most ternary dimensions, collapsed α is com-\n1663\tparable to raw ordinal α; for expressiveness, col-\n1664\tlapsed α exceeds raw α , indicating that the mid-\n1665\tdle level adds noise rather than signal. Three-way\n1666\tsplits on ternary dimensions (each rater assigning a\n1667\tdistinct level) occur only on the 1-versus-2 bound-\n1668\tary and are irresolvable by majority vote; these\n1669\tsamples are excluded from the final dataset for that\n1670\tdimension.\n1671\t\n1672\t\n1673\t===== PAGE 13 =====\n1674\tTable 6: Annotation rubric for all ten speech quality dimensions. Each dimension is described in full in §3.3.\n1675\tDimension\n1676\tScore\n1677\tMeaning\n1678\tPhonetic Accuracy\n1679\t(§6.2.1)\n1680\t1\n1681\tOne or more consonants or vowels are wrong and the error(s) are\n1682\tvery noticeable. You might mishear the word entirely.\n1683\t2\n1684\tOne or more consonants or vowels are slightly off, but you can still\n1685\ttell what word was intended without much effort.\n1686\t3\n1687\tAll consonants and vowels sound correct. No perceivable phonetic\n1688\terrors.\n1689\tLexical Stress\n1690\t(§6.2.2)\n1691\t0\n1692\tAt least one word has stress on the wrong syllable. Even one error\n1693\tearns a 0.\n1694\t1\n1695\tEvery multi-syllable word has stress on the correct syllable with\n1696\tappropriate vowel quality. No errors detected.\n1697\tIntonation (§6.2.3)\n1698\t1\n1699\tOne or more pitch contour errors cause high negative impact. The\n1700\tintonation may confuse the intended sentence type or signal an\n1701\tunintended attitude.\n1702\t2\n1703\tOne or more errors cause low negative impact. Something about\n1704\tthe melody sounds slightly unnatural but does not cause confusion.\n1705\t3\n1706\tNo perceivable errors. The intonation sounds natural and appropri-\n1707\tate for the sentence and its context.\n1708\tProsodic Stress\n1709\t(§6.2.4)\n1710\t0\n1711\tProminence falls on words that do not warrant it, or words that\n1712\tshould be prominent are not.\n1713\t1\n1714\tThe choice of prominent words sounds predictable and appropriate.\n1715\tNew or important information is highlighted; given material is\n1716\tde-emphasized.\n1717\tProsodic Boundary\n1718\tPlacement (§6.2.5)\n1719\t1\n1720\tA boundary is missing where it is needed or present where it should\n1721\tnot be, with severe impact. You cannot recover the intended sen-\n1722\ttence structure while listening.\n1723\t2\n1724\tA boundary is in the wrong position and noticeably hurts the expe-\n1725\trience, but you can still follow the sentence structure.\n1726\t3\n1727\tWords are grouped into phrases that align naturally with the sen-\n1728\ttence’s syntactic and semantic structure.\n1729\tSpeech Rate\n1730\tAppropriateness\n1731\t(§6.2.6)\n1732\t1\n1733\tRate is clearly inappropriate: so fast words blur together, or so slow\n1734\tthe pace feels labored.\n1735\t2\n1736\tRate is slightly too fast, too slow, or too uniform, but you can follow\n1737\tthe content without real difficulty.\n1738\t3\n1739\tTempo feels natural and varies appropriately. Complex material\n1740\tgets more time; parentheticals move briskly.\n1741\tEmotional\n1742\tAppropriateness\n1743\t(§6.2.7)\n1744\t0\n1745\tThe emotional tone is wrong for the content. The mismatch is\n1746\tclearly perceivable.\n1747\t1\n1748\tThe emotional tone is a plausible match for the text and context.\n1749\tExpressiveness\n1750\t(§6.2.8)\n1751\t1\n1752\tDelivery is extremely flat and robotic, or excessively exaggerated,\n1753\tto the point of seriously detracting from the experience.\n1754\t2\n1755\tDelivery is slightly too flat or slightly too dramatic for the content,\n1756\tbut not very distracting.\n1757\t3\n1758\tDelivery has natural, appropriate variation; sounds engaged and\n1759\tdynamic without drawing attention to itself.\n1760\t(Continued on next page)\n1761\t\n1762\t\n1763\t===== PAGE 14 =====\n1764\t(Continued from previous page)\n1765\tDimension\n1766\tScore\n1767\tMeaning\n1768\tSpeaker Identity\n1769\tConsistency (§6.2.9)\n1770\t0\n1771\tThe voice changes in a way that makes it sound like a different\n1772\tperson, either within or across sentences.\n1773\t1\n1774\tThe voice is recognizably consistent throughout. It sounds like a\n1775\tsingle individual, even with natural variation in expressiveness or\n1776\tloudness.\n1777\tHuman Plausibility\n1778\t(§6.2.10)\n1779\t0\n1780\tThe audio contains at least one moment that could not have been pro-\n1781\tduced by a human speaker: robotic or metallic artifacts, impossible\n1782\tpitch jumps, audio glitches, or unnaturally cut-off syllables.\n1783\t1\n1784\tThe entire audio stream sounds like it could have been produced by\n1785\ta human speaker.\n1786\t\n1787\t\n1788\t===== PAGE 15 =====\n1789\t6.2\n1790\tHuman Annotations\n1791\tAnnotator Background\n1792\tAll annotators are first-\n1793\tlanguage English speakers and trained linguists.\n1794\tAnnotators participated voluntarily at the standard\n1795\trate compensation. The annotation task involved\n1796\tlistening to synthesized speech samples and mark-\n1797\ting perceptual quality dimensions based on the\n1798\tlinguistic-verified guidelines; no sensitive or per-\n1799\tsonal data was collected.\n1800\tAnnotator Iteration\n1801\tFor dimensions that con-\n1802\ttain inherent human subjectivity, we initially tested\n1803\tcapturing a severity gradient with a ternary scale\n1804\t(1-3). However, annotators could not reliably dis-\n1805\ttinguish between non-perfect rating levels, so we\n1806\tcollapsed these to binary, trading nuance for dataset\n1807\treliability.\n1808\tSince not all generations reliably exhibit the in-\n1809\ttended failure, we generated an excess of samples\n1810\tand relied on human annotation to establish ground-\n1811\ttruth labels (see Table 9).\n1812\tAnnotation is conducted through a purpose-built\n1813\tinterface presenting the audio, the dimension defi-\n1814\tnition, the revealable transcript, and the response\n1815\toptions in a single screen, minimizing cognitive\n1816\tload and reducing interface-induced variance be-\n1817\ttween raters.\n1818\tAnnotation Guide\n1819\tThe annotation guide pro-\n1820\tvides detailed explanations and rubric ratings on\n1821\thow to rate synthetic (TTS) speech on each metric\n1822\tin the evaluation schema. For every metric, you\n1823\twill find: a plain-language explanation of what the\n1824\tmetric captures, what to listen for, a rating scale\n1825\twith clear descriptions of each score, and examples\n1826\tto anchor annotators’ judgments.\n1827\tThe metrics are grouped into three dimensions:\n1828\tWord-level (are the right sounds and stress patterns\n1829\tproduced for each word?), Prosodic (is the speech\n1830\torganized into phrases with appropriate melody,\n1831\temphasis, and timing?), and Paralinguistic (does\n1832\tthe speech convey appropriate emotion, expressive-\n1833\tness, and speaker characteristics?). The details are\n1834\tprovided in Table 6.\n1835\tGeneral Labeling Guideline\n1836\tListen to each ut-\n1837\tterance at least twice before scoring. On the first\n1838\tpass, get a general impression. On the second pass,\n1839\tfocus on whichever metric you are currently rating.\n1840\tTry not to let a strong error in one metric bias your\n1841\trating in another: a word might be mispronounced\n1842\t(a Phonetic Accuracy issue) while the prosody is\n1843\tperfectly fine, or vice versa. Rate each metric inde-\n1844\tpendently.\n1845\tWord-Level Metrics\n1846\tWord-level metrics ask:\n1847\tdoes each individual word sound right? This covers\n1848\twhether the TTS system picked the correct sounds\n1849\t(phonemes) and placed stress on the correct sylla-\n1850\tble within each word.\n1851\t6.2.1\n1852\tPhonetic Accuracy\n1853\tWhat you are listening for. Does every word con-\n1854\ttain the right speech sounds? This includes conso-\n1855\tnants, vowels, and coarticulation. You are checking\n1856\twhether the system produced the correct phonemes\n1857\tfor each word, especially for tricky cases like het-\n1858\teronyms, rare words, proper nouns, and loanwords.\n1859\tExample. “She will lead the group to the lead\n1860\tmine.” The first lead should rhyme with feed and\n1861\tthe second with red. If the system pronounces both\n1862\tthe same way, that is a phonetic accuracy error.\n1863\tHow disruptive it is determines whether you score\n1864\t1 or 2.\n1865\t6.2.2\n1866\tLexical Stress\n1867\tWhat you are listening for. Is the stress on the right\n1868\tsyllable within each word? For example, the noun\n1869\trecord is stressed on the first syllable (RE-cord)\n1870\twhile the verb is stressed on the second (re-CORD).\n1871\tThis metric is strictly about within-word stress;\n1872\tdo not consider sentence-level emphasis here (see\n1873\tProsodic Stress, Metric 4).\n1874\tWhy binary? Lexical stress errors are relatively\n1875\tunambiguous, and even a single error can substan-\n1876\ttially impair word recognition.\n1877\tLexical vs. prosodic stress. Lexical stress is a\n1878\tfixed dictionary property (which syllable within a\n1879\tword). Prosodic stress (Metric 4) is a speaker-level\n1880\tchoice about which words in a sentence receive\n1881\tprominence.\n1882\tProsodic Metrics\n1883\tProsodic metrics ask: is the\n1884\tspeech organized and delivered in a way that com-\n1885\tmunicates the structure and meaning of the sen-\n1886\ttence?\n1887\tProsodic errors can change what a lis-\n1888\ttener understands even when every individual word\n1889\tsounds correct.\n1890\tBecause pitch, duration, loudness, and pauses\n1891\tserve multiple prosodic functions, a single acoustic\n1892\tevent can be relevant to more than one metric. Rate\n1893\teach metric for its own question, not the acous-\n1894\ttic signal in isolation. Table 7 summarizes which\n1895\tchannels each metric draws on.\n1896\t6.2.3\n1897\tIntonation\n1898\tWhat you are listening for. Intonation is the pitch\n1899\tmelody across phrases and sentences. It signals\n1900\tsentence type (questions typically rise; statements\n1901\t\n1902\t\n1903\t===== PAGE 16 =====\n1904\tTable 7: Acoustic channels for each prosodic metric.\n1905\tMetric\n1906\tPitch\n1907\tDur.\n1908\tLoud.\n1909\tPause\n1910\tIntonation\n1911\t✓\n1912\tProsodic Stress\n1913\t✓\n1914\t✓\n1915\t✓\n1916\tBoundary Placement\n1917\t✓\n1918\t✓\n1919\t✓\n1920\tSpeech Rate\n1921\t✓\n1922\t✓\n1923\tfall), speaker attitude, and discourse structure. This\n1924\tmetric concerns pitch only; do not factor in rate or\n1925\tpauses.\n1926\t6.2.4\n1927\tProsodic Stress\n1928\tWhat you are listening for. Which words in the\n1929\tsentence receive extra prominence? Speakers sig-\n1930\tnal focus via higher pitch, greater loudness, and\n1931\tlonger duration. Do not confuse with lexical stress\n1932\t(Metric 2), which concerns syllables within a word.\n1933\t6.2.5\n1934\tProsodic Boundary Placement\n1935\tWhat you are listening for. Speakers group words\n1936\tinto phrases using pitch movements, final-syllable\n1937\tlengthening, and pauses. Misplaced boundaries\n1938\tdisrupt parsing even when no alternative meaning\n1939\tis at stake. Focus on whether the grouping aligns\n1940\twith plausible syntactic structure and produces a\n1941\tpleasant listening experience.\n1942\tExample. Boundary errors go in both directions.\n1943\tA spurious boundary (“It was a | beautiful day”)\n1944\tsounds choppy; a missing boundary (“When the\n1945\tdog bites the man screams”) creates a parsing fail-\n1946\ture. In both cases the question is whether the\n1947\tboundary made the speech harder or less pleasant\n1948\tto follow.\n1949\t6.2.6\n1950\tSpeech Rate Appropriateness\n1951\tWhat you are listening for. Is the overall tempo\n1952\tappropriate, and does it vary naturally? Human\n1953\tspeakers slow for complex or emotionally weighty\n1954\tmaterial and speed up through predictable or par-\n1955\tenthetical content. This metric is distinct from\n1956\tBoundary Placement (where phrase breaks occur).\n1957\tParalinguistic Metrics\n1958\tParalinguistic metrics\n1959\task: beyond the linguistic message, does the speech\n1960\tconvey appropriate speaker characteristics? This\n1961\tcovers emotion, expressiveness, voice consistency,\n1962\tand whether the audio could have been produced\n1963\tby a human.\n1964\t6.2.7\n1965\tEmotional Appropriateness\n1966\tWhat you are listening for. Does the emotional\n1967\ttone of the voice match the content? A sentence\n1968\tabout exciting news should not sound bored, and\n1969\ta condolence message should not sound cheerful.\n1970\tThis metric does not ask whether the emotion is\n1971\tstrong enough (see Expressiveness, Metric 9); it\n1972\tasks only whether the type of emotion is correct.\n1973\t6.2.8\n1974\tExpressiveness\n1975\tWhat you are listening for. Expressiveness captures\n1976\tthe degree of natural variation in delivery. TTS\n1977\tspeech can be flat (monotone, robotic) or overdone\n1978\t(theatrical, exaggerated). Both extremes are errors.\n1979\tRate expressiveness relative to what is appropri-\n1980\tate for the content: informational material calls\n1981\tfor more measured delivery; emotionally charged\n1982\tcontent warrants greater vocal variation.\n1983\t6.2.9\n1984\tSpeaker Identity Consistency\n1985\tWhat you are listening for. Does the voice maintain\n1986\ta consistent perceived identity (stable pitch range,\n1987\tvocal quality, perceived age, perceived gender, and\n1988\taccent) both within a single sentence and across\n1989\tsentences in a set? Inconsistencies can occur across\n1990\tsentences or partway through a single sentence.\n1991\t6.2.10\n1992\tHuman Plausibility\n1993\tWhat you are listening for. This metric is inde-\n1994\tpendent of linguistic correctness. The question\n1995\tis whether a human being could have physically\n1996\tproduced this audio. It targets digital artifacts:\n1997\trobotic buzzing, clicks, metallic resonance, im-\n1998\tpossible pitch jumps, abruptly cut-off syllables,\n1999\tor anything outside the range of human vocal pro-\n2000\tduction. Do not penalize linguistic errors here; a\n2001\tmispronounced word is a Phonetic Accuracy issue\n2002\tbut may still sound physically human.\n2003\tQuick Reference\n2004\tTable 8: All metrics at a glance.\n2005\tDim.\n2006\tMetric\n2007\tScale\n2008\tSummary\n2009\tWord\n2010\tPhonetic Accuracy\n2011\t1–3\n2012\tRight sounds produced?\n2013\tLexical Stress\n2014\t0/1\n2015\tRight syllable stressed?\n2016\tPros.\n2017\tIntonation\n2018\t1–3\n2019\tPitch melody fits sen-\n2020\ttence?\n2021\tProsodic Stress\n2022\t0/1\n2023\tRight\n2024\twords\n2025\tempha-\n2026\tsized?\n2027\tBoundary Placement\n2028\t1–3\n2029\tNatural phrase group-\n2030\ting?\n2031\tSpeech Rate\n2032\t1–3\n2033\tTempo appropriate?\n2034\tPara.\n2035\tEmotional Appr.\n2036\t0/1\n2037\tEmotion matches con-\n2038\ttent?\n2039\tExpressiveness\n2040\t1–3\n2041\tDelivery\n2042\tdynamic/fit-\n2043\tting?\n2044\tSpeaker Consistency\n2045\t0/1\n2046\tSame person through-\n2047\tout?\n2048\tHuman Plausibility\n2049\t0/1\n2050\tCould a human produce\n2051\tthis?\n2052\tRemember: Rate each metric independently. A\n2053\tsingle utterance can score 3 on Phonetic Accuracy\n2054\tbut 1 on Intonation, or 0 on Human Plausibility but\n2055\t1 on Lexical Stress. Do not let a strong impression\n2056\tfrom one dimension bleed into another.\n2057\t\n2058\t\n2059\t===== PAGE 17 =====\n2060\t6.3\n2061\tAcoustic Overlap in the Prosodic and\n2062\tParalinguistic Tiers\n2063\tThe prosodic dimensions share acoustic channels:\n2064\tf0, for instance, carries intonation, prosodic stress,\n2065\tand boundary tones simultaneously.\n2066\tCrucially,\n2067\thowever, sharing an acoustic channel does not en-\n2068\ttail these dimensions cannot fail independently. A\n2069\tmisplaced prosodic boundary may disrupt the pitch\n2070\tcontour without any corresponding failure in ac-\n2071\tcent placement, and a stress error may occur with\n2072\tan otherwise well-formed intonation contour. This\n2073\tindependence motivates rating each dimension sep-\n2074\tarately rather than holistically: each stimulus is\n2075\trated on all applicable dimensions independently,\n2076\tso cases where a single acoustic anomaly produces\n2077\tfailures across multiple categories are captured ex-\n2078\tplicitly rather than collapsed into a single quality\n2079\tjudgment.\n2080\tThe paralinguistic tier presents an analogous\n2081\tcase. These four dimensions share acoustic chan-\n2082\tnels with prosody but convey information about the\n2083\tspeaker rather than the linguistic message. A syn-\n2084\tthesis artifact may simultaneously affect perceived\n2085\tspeaker identity consistency and human plausibil-\n2086\tity, yet the two dimensions target distinct speaker\n2087\tproperties and can fail independently of one an-\n2088\tother. Figure 2 further demonstrates the idea of\n2089\tcorrelations.\n2090\t6.4\n2091\tParalinguistic Framework and Neural\n2092\tTTS\n2093\tCrystal’s original framework distinguishes voice\n2094\tquality features, which are speaker-stable proper-\n2095\tties and remain constant across an utterance, from\n2096\taffective paralinguistic features, which modulate\n2097\tin response to content and context. The four par-\n2098\talinguistic dimensions in our schema map directly\n2099\tonto this distinction: speaker identity consistency\n2100\tand human plausibility reflect speaker-stable prop-\n2101\terties, while expressiveness and emotional appro-\n2102\tpriateness reflect content-driven modulation.\n2103\tHuman plausibility warrants particular note.\n2104\tModern neural TTS can produce voices that are\n2105\tinternally consistent and expressively adequate but\n2106\tphysically implausible (a failure mode not antici-\n2107\tpated by Crystal’s framework but characteristic of\n2108\tneural synthesis). We treat it as a distinct paralin-\n2109\tguistic dimension rather than subsuming it under\n2110\toverall quality, since it represents a novel category\n2111\tof synthesis failure with no natural analog in human\n2112\tspeech perception research.\n2113\t6.5\n2114\tIntended versus observed labels.\n2115\tTable 9 reports the agreement between intended\n2116\tquality levels (design variable) and majority-vote\n2117\tannotator labels (ground truth). Of 741 samples\n2118\tintended to contain the targeted failure, annotators\n2119\tmajority-labeled 239 (32.3%) as having no fail-\n2120\ture: approximately 1 in 3 designed errors was not\n2121\tperceived as such. Misses are highest for emo-\n2122\ttional appropriateness (46.2%), intonation (40.0%),\n2123\tand lexical stress (38.4%), and lowest for prosodic\n2124\tboundary placement (22.4%) and phonetic accu-\n2125\tracy (21.2%). The false-alarm rate on intended-\n2126\tclean items is only 3.5%, indicating that raters sys-\n2127\ttematically under-call failures rather than over-call\n2128\tthem which is a pattern consistent with the dif-\n2129\tficulty of eliciting perceptually salient errors on\n2130\thigher-level dimensions.\n2131\t6.6\n2132\tAUROC Analysis for MOS Models\n2133\tAs demonstrated in Table 10, MOS models ex-\n2134\thibit striking performance gap across dimensions.\n2135\tMost presented models achieve strong alignment\n2136\twith human judgments on human plausibility (AUC\n2137\t0.72 −0.92) and speech rate (AUC 0.40 −0.89),\n2138\tyet systematically fail on word-level and lower\n2139\tprosodic dimensions. Phonetic accuracy and lexi-\n2140\tcal stress yield near-chance performance across all\n2141\tmodels (AUC 0.41 −0.61), while intonation and\n2142\tprosodic stress show modest, inconsistent correla-\n2143\ttion (AUC 0.33 −0.74).\n2144\t6.7\n2145\tPrompt Details\n2146\tCondition 1: Underspecified MOS-style prompt\n2147\t[ROLE] You are a speech quality evaluator\n2148\tassessing text-to-speech audio.\n2149\t[TASK] % if show_text %A synthesized speech\n2150\tsample is attached above, along with the text\n2151\tit was generated from. Evaluate the overall\n2152\tnaturalness of the speech.\n2153\tIntended\n2154\ttext:\n2155\t\"original_text\"%\n2156\telse\n2157\t%A\n2158\tsynthesized speech sample is attached above.\n2159\tEvaluate its overall naturalness based on the\n2160\taudio alone.% endif %\n2161\t[CRITERIA] Rate the naturalness on a binary\n2162\tscale (0 = failure present, 1 = no failure):\n2163\t0: One or more issues affecting naturalness\n2164\tare perceivable, whether minor or severe. 1:\n2165\tCompletely\n2166\tnatural,\n2167\tindistinguishable\n2168\tfrom\n2169\thuman speech.\n2170\t[PROCEDURE] Follow these steps: 1. Attend to\n2171\tthe attached audio sample.\n2172\t% if show_text\n2173\t%2. Compare the spoken output to the intended\n2174\ttext.\n2175\t3.\n2176\tIdentify any issues that affect\n2177\tnaturalness. 4. Decide the most appropriate\n2178\tscore.% else %2.\n2179\tIdentify any issues that\n2180\t\n2181\t\n2182\t===== PAGE 18 =====\n2183\tTable 9: Intended versus observed label distributions on targeted samples. Caught = majority vote agrees with\n2184\tintended failure. Missed = majority vote labels intended failure as clean. Tie = no majority among the first 3 valid\n2185\tbinary votes (only possible when fewer than 3 raters scored the dimension). Miss rate = missed / intended-fail.\n2186\tBottom panel: of 376 intended-clean items, 363 (96.5%) were observed clean; false-alarm rate = 3.5%.\n2187\tTier\n2188\tDimension\n2189\tN\n2190\tInt. fail\n2191\tCaught\n2192\tMissed\n2193\tTie\n2194\tMiss rate\n2195\tWord-level\n2196\tPhonetic accuracy\n2197\t120\n2198\t80\n2199\t63\n2200\t17\n2201\t0\n2202\t21.2%\n2203\tLexical stress\n2204\t113\n2205\t73\n2206\t45\n2207\t28\n2208\t0\n2209\t38.4%\n2210\tProsodic\n2211\tIntonation\n2212\t120\n2213\t80\n2214\t44\n2215\t32\n2216\t4\n2217\t40.0%\n2218\tProsodic stress\n2219\t124\n2220\t79\n2221\t53\n2222\t26\n2223\t0\n2224\t32.9%\n2225\tProsodic boundary placement\n2226\t95\n2227\t58\n2228\t45\n2229\t13\n2230\t0\n2231\t22.4%\n2232\tSpeech rate\n2233\t168\n2234\t128\n2235\t93\n2236\t35\n2237\t0\n2238\t27.3%\n2239\tParalinguistic\n2240\tEmotional appropriateness\n2241\t58\n2242\t39\n2243\t21\n2244\t18\n2245\t0\n2246\t46.2%\n2247\tExpressiveness\n2248\t100\n2249\t70\n2250\t50\n2251\t20\n2252\t0\n2253\t28.6%\n2254\tSpeaker identity consistency\n2255\t104\n2256\t64\n2257\t32\n2258\t31\n2259\t1\n2260\t48.4%\n2261\tHuman plausibility\n2262\t115\n2263\t70\n2264\t49\n2265\t19\n2266\t2\n2267\t27.1%\n2268\tTotal\n2269\t1117\n2270\t741\n2271\t495\n2272\t239\n2273\t7\n2274\t32.3%\n2275\tIntended-clean items (n = 376): observed clean 363 (96.5%); false-alarm rate 3.5%; ties 0.\n2276\tTable 10: Construct-aligned AUC for MOS models per evaluation dimension.\n2277\tModel\n2278\tWord\n2279\tProsodic\n2280\tParalinguistic\n2281\tPhon. Lex.\n2282\tInton.\n2283\tPros.S\n2284\tPros.B Rate\n2285\tEmo. Expr.\n2286\tSpk.\n2287\tHum.\n2288\tAudioBoxCE\n2289\t0.46\n2290\t0.61\n2291\t0.76∗∗∗\n2292\t0.33∗∗\n2293\t0.47\n2294\t0.89∗∗∗\n2295\t0.53\n2296\t0.63∗\n2297\t0.31∗∗\n2298\t0.92∗∗∗\n2299\tAudioBoxCU\n2300\t0.46\n2301\t0.51\n2302\t0.57\n2303\t0.55\n2304\t0.53\n2305\t0.84∗∗∗\n2306\t0.48\n2307\t0.79∗∗∗\n2308\t0.38\n2309\t0.86∗∗∗\n2310\tAudioBoxPC\n2311\t0.46\n2312\t0.49\n2313\t0.43\n2314\t0.74∗∗∗\n2315\t0.61\n2316\t0.51\n2317\t0.47\n2318\t0.73∗∗∗\n2319\t0.40\n2320\t0.07∗∗∗\n2321\tAudioBoxPQ\n2322\t0.44\n2323\t0.53\n2324\t0.69∗∗\n2325\t0.38∗\n2326\t0.44\n2327\t0.81∗∗∗\n2328\t0.51\n2329\t0.71∗∗∗\n2330\t0.23∗∗∗\n2331\t0.92∗∗∗\n2332\tDNSMOS-ProBVCC\n2333\t0.42\n2334\t0.46\n2335\t0.56\n2336\t0.59\n2337\t0.63\n2338\t0.40∗\n2339\t0.46\n2340\t0.45\n2341\t0.36\n2342\t0.82∗∗∗\n2343\tDNSMOS-ProVCC\n2344\t0.50\n2345\t0.41\n2346\t0.52\n2347\t0.47\n2348\t0.47\n2349\t0.55\n2350\t0.59\n2351\t0.79∗∗∗\n2352\t0.44\n2353\t0.78∗∗∗\n2354\tNISQA\n2355\t0.52\n2356\t0.47\n2357\t0.55\n2358\t0.60\n2359\t0.57\n2360\t0.67∗∗\n2361\t0.49\n2362\t0.74∗∗∗\n2363\t0.47\n2364\t0.76∗∗∗\n2365\tUTMOSv2\n2366\t0.50\n2367\t0.56\n2368\t0.70∗∗\n2369\t0.64∗\n2370\t0.48\n2371\t0.77∗∗∗\n2372\t0.46\n2373\t0.57\n2374\t0.53\n2375\t0.72∗∗\n2376\tAudioBox subscripts: CE = Content Enjoyment; CU = Content Usefulness; PC = Production Complexity; PQ = Production Quality.\n2377\tDNSMOS-Pro subscripts: BVCC = trained on BVCC corpus; VCC = trained on VCC2018 corpus. Column key: Phon. = Phonetic\n2378\tAccuracy; Lex. = Lexical Stress; Inton. = Intonation; Pros.S = Prosodic Stress; Pros.B = Prosodic Boundary; Rate = Speech Rate;\n2379\tEmo. = Emotional Appropriateness; Expr. = Expressiveness; Spk. = Speaker Identity; Hum. = Human Plausibility. Bold =\n2380\tsignificant (p < .05). ∗p < .05, ∗∗p < .01, ∗∗∗p < .001 (two-sided Mann–Whitney U).\n2381\taffect\n2382\tnaturalness.\n2383\t3.\n2384\tDecide\n2385\tthe\n2386\tmost\n2387\tappropriate score.% endif %\n2388\t[OUTPUT] Respond in this exact JSON format:\n2389\t\"evaluation_steps\":\n2390\t\"<your\n2391\tstep-by-step\n2392\treasoning>\", \"score\": <0 or 1>\n2393\tCondition 2a: Schema-guided prompt, single\n2394\t[ROLE]\n2395\tYou\n2396\tare\n2397\tan\n2398\texpert\n2399\tspeech\n2400\tquality\n2401\tevaluator\n2402\twith\n2403\ttraining\n2404\tin\n2405\tphonetics\n2406\tand\n2407\tprosodic phonology.\n2408\t[TASK] % if show_text %A synthesized speech\n2409\tsample\n2410\tis\n2411\tattached\n2412\tabove,\n2413\talong\n2414\twith\n2415\tthe\n2416\ttext it was generated from.\n2417\tEvaluate the\n2418\toverall quality of the speech, considering all\n2419\tdimensions in the evaluation schema below.\n2420\tIntended\n2421\ttext:\n2422\t\"original_text\"%\n2423\telse\n2424\t%A\n2425\tsynthesized speech sample is attached above.\n2426\tEvaluate the overall quality of the speech,\n2427\tconsidering all dimensions in the evaluation\n2428\tschema below.% endif %\n2429\t[CRITERIA]\n2430\tConsider\n2431\tthe\n2432\tfollowing\n2433\tten\n2434\tdimensions when forming your overall judgment:\n2435\tWORD-LEVEL:\n2436\t-\n2437\tPhonetic\n2438\tAccuracy:\n2439\tDoes\n2440\tevery word contain the right speech sounds?\n2441\tThis\n2442\tincludes\n2443\tconsonants,\n2444\tvowels,\n2445\tand\n2446\tthe\n2447\tway\n2448\tadjacent\n2449\tsounds\n2450\tblend\n2451\ttogether\n2452\t(coarticulation).\n2453\tCheck whether the system\n2454\tproduced the correct phonemes for each word,\n2455\tespecially for heteronyms, rare words, proper\n2456\tnouns, and loanwords.\n2457\t- Lexical Stress: Is\n2458\tthe stress on the right syllable within each\n2459\tword? Every multi-syllable English word has a\n2460\tpredictable stress pattern. Incorrect lexical\n2461\tstress often changes vowel quality too. This\n2462\tis strictly about within-word stress.\n2463\tPROSODIC: - Intonation: The melody of speech:\n2464\tthe way pitch rises and falls across phrases\n2465\tand\n2466\tsentences.\n2467\tIt\n2468\tsignals\n2469\tsentence\n2470\ttype,\n2471\tspeaker\n2472\tattitude,\n2473\tand\n2474\tdiscourse\n2475\tstructure.\n2476\tConcerns pitch only. - Prosodic Stress: Which\n2477\twords in a sentence receive extra prominence\n2478\tvia\n2479\thigher\n2480\tpitch,\n2481\tgreater\n2482\tloudness,\n2483\tand\n2484\tlonger duration, signaling which information\n2485\tis\n2486\tnew,\n2487\timportant,\n2488\tor\n2489\tcontrastive.\n2490\tThis\n2491\tis\n2492\tabout\n2493\tentire\n2494\twords\n2495\twithin\n2496\ta\n2497\tsentence,\n2498\tnot\n2499\tsyllables\n2500\twithin\n2501\ta\n2502\tword.\n2503\t-\n2504\tProsodic\n2505\tBoundary\n2506\tPlacement:\n2507\tSpeakers\n2508\tgroup\n2509\twords\n2510\t\n2511\t\n2512\t===== PAGE 19 =====\n2513\tinto phrases using pitch movements,\n2514\tslight\n2515\tlengthening of the final syllable, and pauses.\n2516\tMisplaced\n2517\tboundaries\n2518\tdisrupt\n2519\tthe\n2520\tlistening\n2521\texperience. Focus on whether grouping aligns\n2522\twith plausible syntactic structure. - Speech\n2523\tRate Appropriateness:\n2524\tIs the overall speed\n2525\tappropriate,\n2526\tand\n2527\tdoes\n2528\tit\n2529\tvary\n2530\tin\n2531\tnatural\n2532\tways?\n2533\tHuman speakers slow down for complex\n2534\tmaterial\n2535\tand\n2536\tspeed\n2537\tup\n2538\tthrough\n2539\tpredictable\n2540\tmaterial.\n2541\tPARALINGUISTIC: - Emotional Appropriateness:\n2542\tDoes the emotional tone of the voice match\n2543\tthe content of the text?\n2544\tThis does not ask\n2545\twhether the emotion is strong enough (that\n2546\tis Expressiveness). It asks only whether the\n2547\ttype of emotion is correct. - Expressiveness:\n2548\tNatural variation in delivery style,\n2549\tpace,\n2550\tand vocal energy.\n2551\tHuman speech is dynamic.\n2552\tTTS speech can be flat or overdone.\n2553\tRate\n2554\trelative to what would be appropriate for the\n2555\tcontent. - Speaker Identity Consistency: Does\n2556\tthe\n2557\tvoice\n2558\tmaintain\n2559\ta\n2560\tconsistent\n2561\tperceived\n2562\tidentity\n2563\tin\n2564\tterms\n2565\tof\n2566\tpitch\n2567\trange,\n2568\tvocal\n2569\tquality, perceived age, gender, and accent?\n2570\tCheck\n2571\tfor\n2572\tshifts\n2573\tboth\n2574\twithin\n2575\tand\n2576\tacross\n2577\tsentences.\n2578\t- Human Plausibility:\n2579\tCould a\n2580\thuman have physically produced this audio?\n2581\tTargets\n2582\tdigital\n2583\tartifacts\n2584\tand\n2585\tglitches:\n2586\trobotic buzzing, clicks, unnatural metallic\n2587\tresonance,\n2588\timpossible\n2589\tpitch\n2590\tjumps,\n2591\tabrupt\n2592\tsyllable cutoffs.\n2593\tIndependent of linguistic\n2594\tcorrectness.\n2595\tConsidering ALL of the above, rate overall\n2596\tquality\n2597\ton\n2598\ta\n2599\tbinary\n2600\tscale\n2601\t(0\n2602\t=\n2603\tfailure\n2604\tpresent, 1 = no failure):\n2605\t0:\n2606\tOne or more\n2607\tissues\n2608\tdetected\n2609\tacross\n2610\tthe\n2611\tten\n2612\tdimensions,\n2613\twhether minor or severe.\n2614\t1: No perceivable\n2615\tissues detected across all ten dimensions.\n2616\t[PROCEDURE] Follow these steps: 1. Attend to\n2617\tthe attached audio sample. 2. For each of the\n2618\tthree dimension groups (word-level, prosodic,\n2619\tparalinguistic),\n2620\tnote\n2621\twhether\n2622\tany\n2623\tissues\n2624\tare\n2625\tpresent.\n2626\t3.\n2627\tIdentify\n2628\twhich\n2629\tspecific\n2630\tdimensions (if any) show failures. 4. Decide\n2631\tthe most appropriate score.\n2632\t[OUTPUT] Respond in this exact JSON format:\n2633\t\"word_level_issues\": \"<any word-level issues\n2634\tnoted>\",\n2635\t\"prosodic_issues\":\n2636\t\"<any prosodic\n2637\tissues\n2638\tnoted>\",\n2639\t\"paralinguistic_issues\":\n2640\t\"<any paralinguistic issues noted>\", \"score\":\n2641\t<0 or 1>\n2642\tCondition 2b: Schema-guided prompt, per-dimension\n2643\tscores\n2644\t[ROLE]\n2645\tYou\n2646\tare\n2647\tan\n2648\texpert\n2649\tspeech\n2650\tquality\n2651\tevaluator\n2652\twith\n2653\ttraining\n2654\tin\n2655\tphonetics\n2656\tand\n2657\tprosodic phonology.\n2658\t[TASK] % if show_text %A synthesized speech\n2659\tsample is attached above, along with the text\n2660\tit was generated from.\n2661\tEvaluate the speech\n2662\tindependently on each of the ten dimensions\n2663\tbelow, using the specified scale for each.\n2664\tIntended\n2665\ttext:\n2666\t\"original_text\"%\n2667\telse\n2668\t%A\n2669\tsynthesized speech sample is attached above.\n2670\tEvaluate the speech independently on each of\n2671\tthe ten dimensions below, using the specified\n2672\tscale for each.% endif %\n2673\t[CRITERIA]\n2674\tRate\n2675\teach\n2676\tmetric\n2677\tindependently.\n2678\tDo\n2679\tnot\n2680\tlet\n2681\ta\n2682\tstrong\n2683\timpression\n2684\tfrom\n2685\tone\n2686\tdimension bleed into another.\n2687\tALL DIMENSIONS use a binary scale (0 = failure\n2688\tpresent, 1 = no failure):\n2689\tLexical Stress:\n2690\tIs the stress on the right\n2691\tsyllable within each word? This is strictly\n2692\tabout within-word stress, not sentence-level\n2693\temphasis. 0: At least one word has stress on\n2694\tthe wrong syllable. 1: Every multi-syllable\n2695\tword has stress on the correct syllable.\n2696\tProsodic Stress: Which words in the sentence\n2697\treceive\n2698\textra\n2699\tprominence\n2700\tvia\n2701\thigher\n2702\tpitch,\n2703\tgreater\n2704\tloudness,\n2705\tand\n2706\tlonger\n2707\tduration?\n2708\t0:\n2709\tProminence falls on words that do not\n2710\twarrant it, or words that should be prominent\n2711\tare\n2712\tnot.\n2713\t1:\n2714\tThe\n2715\tchoice\n2716\tof\n2717\twhich\n2718\twords\n2719\treceive\n2720\tprominence\n2721\tsounds\n2722\tpredictable\n2723\tand\n2724\tappropriate.\n2725\tEmotional Appropriateness:\n2726\tDoes the emotion\n2727\tof the voice match the content of the text?\n2728\tIf the text is neutral, the emotion should be\n2729\tneutral as well. 0: The emotion is wrong for\n2730\tthe content.\n2731\t1: The emotion is a plausible\n2732\tmatch for the text.\n2733\tSpeaker Identity Consistency: Does the voice\n2734\tmaintain\n2735\ta\n2736\tconsistent\n2737\tperceived\n2738\tidentity?\n2739\t0: The voice changes in a way that makes it\n2740\tsound like a different person. 1: The voice\n2741\tis recognizably consistent throughout.\n2742\tHuman\n2743\tPlausibility:\n2744\tCould\n2745\ta\n2746\thuman\n2747\thave\n2748\tphysically produced this audio? 0: The audio\n2749\tcontains at least one moment that could not\n2750\thave been produced by a human speaker. 1: The\n2751\tentire audio stream sounds like it could have\n2752\tbeen produced by a human speaker.\n2753\tPhonetic Accuracy: 0: One or more consonants\n2754\tor vowels are outside the range of possible\n2755\texpected sounds for the utterance spoken in\n2756\tgeneral american english. 1: All consonants\n2757\tand vowels fall within the range of possible\n2758\texpected sounds for the utterance spoken in\n2759\tgeneral american english.\n2760\tIntonation:\n2761\t0:\n2762\tPerceivable\n2763\terrors\n2764\tin\n2765\tthe\n2766\tpitch\n2767\tcontour,\n2768\tregardless\n2769\tof\n2770\tseverity.\n2771\t1: No perceivable errors in the pitch contour.\n2772\tProsodic Boundary Placement:\n2773\t0:\n2774\tMisplaced\n2775\tboundary\n2776\tdisrupts\n2777\tsentence\n2778\tstructure,\n2779\tnoticeably or severely.\n2780\t1:\n2781\tPhrase grouping\n2782\taligns naturally with syntactic structure.\n2783\tSpeech Rate Appropriateness: 0: Speech rate\n2784\tis too slow or too fast during parts or the\n2785\twhole of the utterance. 1: Speech rate feels\n2786\tnatural and varies appropriately.\n2787\t\n2788\t\n2789\t===== PAGE 20 =====\n2790\tExpressiveness:\n2791\t0:\n2792\tDelivery\n2793\tis\n2794\ttoo\n2795\tflat\n2796\tor\n2797\ttoo\n2798\tdramatic,\n2799\tregardless\n2800\tof\n2801\tseverity.\n2802\t1:\n2803\tDelivery\n2804\thas\n2805\tnatural, appropriate variation.\n2806\t[PROCEDURE] For each dimension: 1. Attend to\n2807\tthe attached audio sample focusing on that\n2808\tdimension.\n2809\t2.\n2810\tCompare what you hear to the\n2811\trubric anchors above. 3. Record your reasoning\n2812\tand score.\n2813\tEvaluate each dimension independently.\n2814\t[OUTPUT] Respond in this exact JSON format:\n2815\t{\n2816\t\"phonetic_accuracy\":{\"reasoning\": \"...\", \"score\": <0 or\n2817\t1>},\n2818\t\"lexical_stress\":{\"reasoning\": \"...\", \"score\": <0 or\n2819\t1>},\n2820\t\"intonation\": {\"reasoning\": \"...\", \"score\": <0 or 1>},\n2821\t\"prosodic_stress\":{\"reasoning\": \"...\", \"score\": <0 or\n2822\t1>},\n2823\t\"prosodic_boundary\":{\"reasoning\": \"...\", \"score\": <0 or\n2824\t1>},\n2825\t\"speech_rate\": {\"reasoning\": \"...\", \"score\": <0 or 1>},\n2826\t\"emotional_appropriateness\":\n2827\t{\"reasoning\": \"...\", \"\n2828\tscore\": <0 or 1>},\n2829\t\"expressiveness\":{\"reasoning\": \"...\", \"score\": <0 or\n2830\t1>},\n2831\t\"speaker_identity\":{\"reasoning\": \"...\", \"score\": <0 or\n2832\t1>},\n2833\t\"human_plausibility\":{\"reasoning\": \"...\", \"score\": <0\n2834\tor 1>}\n2835\t}\n2836\tCondition 3: Isolated per-dimension prompts\n2837\t[ROLE]\n2838\tYou\n2839\tare\n2840\tan\n2841\texpert\n2842\tspeech\n2843\tquality\n2844\tevaluator\n2845\twith\n2846\ttraining\n2847\tin\n2848\tphonetics\n2849\tand\n2850\tprosodic phonology.\n2851\t[TASK] % if show_text %A synthesized speech\n2852\tsample is attached above, along with the text\n2853\tit was generated from. Evaluate the speech on\n2854\tONE specific dimension.\n2855\tIntended\n2856\ttext:\n2857\t\"original_text\"%\n2858\telse\n2859\t%A\n2860\tsynthesized speech sample is attached above.\n2861\tEvaluate the speech on ONE specific dimension\n2862\tbased on the audio alone.% endif %\n2863\t[CRITERIA] dimension_block\n2864\t[PROCEDURE] Follow these steps:\n2865\t1.\n2866\tAttend\n2867\tto\n2868\tthe\n2869\tattached\n2870\taudio\n2871\tsample.\n2872\t2.\n2873\tFocus\n2874\texclusively on dimension_name.\n2875\t3.\n2876\tCompare\n2877\twhat you hear to the score anchors above. 4.\n2878\tSelect your score.\n2879\tDo not consider any other aspect of speech\n2880\tquality. Evaluate ONLY dimension_name.\n2881\t[OUTPUT]\n2882\tRespond\n2883\tin\n2884\tthis\n2885\texact\n2886\tJSON\n2887\tformat:\n2888\t\"reasoning\": \"<your reasoning about\n2889\tdimension_name>\", \"score\": <score_range>\n2890\t7\n2891\tGeneration Pipeline Details\n2892\tThis appendix documents the model configura-\n2893\ttions and hyperparameters for the error genera-\n2894\ttion pipeline described in Section 3.2. All audio:\n2895\t44,100 Hz, 16-bit PCM, mono. All dimensions use\n2896\tbinary labels: 1 (acceptable) and 0 (error). For\n2897\tdimensions originally generated with three severity\n2898\tlevels, scores 1 and 2 were collapsed to label 0;\n2899\tscore 3 maps to label 1.\n2900\t7.1\n2901\tShared Defaults\n2902\tLLM. Azure OpenAI gpt-5;\n2903\tapi_version\n2904\t2025-03-01-preview; max_completion_tokens\n2905\t= 16384; temperature: API default.\n2906\tTTS\n2907\t(default).\n2908\tCartesia\n2909\tsonic-3\n2910\t(Blake:\n2911\ta167e0f3-df7e-4d52-a9c3-f949145efdab, Brooke:\n2912\te07c00bc-4134-4eae-9ea4-1a55fb45746b)\n2913\tTTS\n2914\t(intonation).\n2915\tElevenLabs\n2916\televen_multilingual_v2.\n2917\t7.2\n2918\tPathway 1: IPA-Controlled TTS\n2919\tgpt-5 produces a modified transcript for each in-\n2920\ttended error level, passed to Cartesia Sonic-3. Ta-\n2921\tble 11 lists voices and prompts are listed in 7.5;\n2922\tTable 12 lists intended errors.\n2923\tTable 11: IPA pathway: voice per dimension. All use\n2924\tCartesia Sonic-3.\n2925\tDim.\n2926\tVoice\n2927\tPhon. Acc.\n2928\tBrooke\n2929\tLex. Str.\n2930\tBlake\n2931\tPros. Str.\n2932\tBlake\n2933\tTable 12: IPA pathway: intended error per label.\n2934\tDim.\n2935\tLabel\n2936\tIntended error\n2937\tPhon.\n2938\t1\n2939\tCanonical IPA\n2940\tAcc.\n2941\t0\n2942\tPhoneme\n2943\tsubstitution\n2944\t(within- or cross-class)\n2945\tLex.\n2946\t1\n2947\tCanonical stress\n2948\tStr.\n2949\t0\n2950\tShifted stress + vowel reduc-\n2951\ttion\n2952\tPros.\n2953\t1\n2954\tEmphasis on content word\n2955\tStr.\n2956\t0\n2957\tEmphasis on function word\n2958\t(Cartesia quote-markup)\n2959\t7.3\n2960\tPathway 2: Acoustic Manipulation\n2961\tA clean TTS baseline is synthesised first; Parsel-\n2962\tmouth/Praat PSOLA or local DSP then introduces\n2963\ta targeted perturbation.\n2964\tIntonation.\n2965\tSource:\n2966\tEmergentTTS-Questions\n2967\tsplit (declaratives and questions; 4–12 words;\n2968\tno commas/dashes).\n2969\tElevenLabs voice Brian\n2970\t(nPczCjzI2devNBz1zQrb) serves as the label-1 an-\n2971\tchor; label-0 samples are derived via F0 manipula-\n2972\ttion with the Praat parameters in Table 13.\n2973\tLabel 1: verbatim ElevenLabs synthesis. Label\n2974\t0: F0 contour manipulation ; either terminal-rise\n2975\tcompression (last ∼30% of contour compressed to\n2976\t15–30% of original excursion) or one of 10 high-\n2977\timpact strategies (Table 14), randomly selected.\n2978\t\n2979\t\n2980\t===== PAGE 21 =====\n2981\tTable 13: Praat PSOLA parameters (Intonation and\n2982\tSpeaker Identity).\n2983\tParameter\n2984\tValue\n2985\tAnalysis floor\n2986\t75 Hz\n2987\tManipulation floor\n2988\t50 Hz\n2989\tPitch ceiling\n2990\t600 Hz\n2991\tOutput clip range\n2992\t60–320 Hz\n2993\tControl points\n2994\t45\n2995\tTime step\n2996\t0.01 s\n2997\tTwo strategies may be stacked with probability\n2998\t–stack-rate P (default 0.0).\n2999\tTable 14: F0 strategies for Intonation label 0. All oper-\n3000\tate within mean ± 70–130 Hz.\n3001\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "356455f0-e3c6-4e87-9d48-a787cab68dc3", "uuid": "cf4c5bbb-6c4a-4a06-a9ae-4aa5c16768ae", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T08:32:07.742Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/356455f0-e3c6-4e87-9d48-a787cab68dc3/tool-results/bpe7wne4w.txt", "content": "Pages: 27\n\n===== PAGE 1 =====\nBeyond Naturalness: Probing Automated Text-To-Speech Evaluators on\nLinguistically Grounded Dimensions\nOluwanifemi Bamgbose*, Simon Rosen, Jash Shah,\nLindsay Devon Brin, Hoang H Nguyen, Anke Koelzer, Rachel Hansen,\nTara Bogavelli, Fanny Riols\nServiceNow\nnifemi.bamgbose@servicenow.com\nAbstract\nAutomated Text-to-Speech (TTS) evaluation\nmethods (Mean Opinion Score (MOS) predic-\ntors and Audio Large Language Models (Audio-\nLLMs) judges) are expected to reflect human\nperception, yet it is unclear how well they cap-\nture the distinct aspects of speech that listen-\ners actually perceive.\nWe deconstruct \"nat-\nuralness\" into a linguistically grounded an-\nnotation schema spanning 10 distinct percep-\ntual dimensions, and use it to construct the\nfirst dimension-level meta-evaluation bench-\nmark for TTS, comprising 860 utterances an-\nnotated by trained linguist raters. Results from\nbenchmarking four MOS predictors and four\nAudio-LLMs judges reveal that MOS predic-\ntors collapse onto acoustic signal quality, while\nAudio-LLMs judges show selective, prompt-\ndependent detection that does not generalise\nacross all dimensions. Neither class reliably\ncaptures a breadth of linguistically structured\nspeech errors. Our dataset, annotation schema,\nand evaluation code are publicly released to\nsupport more targeted and interpretable TTS\nevaluation.\n1\nIntroduction\nText-to-speech (TTS) systems are increasingly de-\nployed in production contexts where the natural-\nness of synthesized speech directly affects user\nexperience and comprehension, with potential con-\nsequences for user engagement (Tan et al., 2021;\nXie et al., 2025). Listeners are sensitive to a mul-\ntidimensional set of failures; a TTS system may\nstress the wrong syllable in a word, break a sen-\ntence at an unnatural point, or deliver a question\nwith the flat intonation of a statement, any of which\nwould be immediately human-perceptible (Geneva\net al., 2023; Gutierrez et al., 2021; Lee et al., 2026).\nThe gold standard for measuring TTS quality\nacross these dimensions remains human perceptual\njudgment (Baughan et al., 2023; Ulgen et al., 2026).\n* Corresponding author.\nTable 1:\nCapability comparison with recent meta-\nevaluation resources. ETE: EmergentTTS-Eval (Manku\net al., 2025); ITE: InstructTTSEval (Huang et al., 2025);\nSJ: SpeechJudge (Zhang et al., 2025); SS: Speaker-\nSleuth (Lee et al., 2026). ✓present, ~ partial, ✗absent;\npartial = human labels only validate a model judge\n(ETE/ITE), or controlled difficulty without injected lin-\nguistic errors (SS). Only ours pairs expert, aspect-level\nhuman labels with a linguistically-grounded taxonomy\nand controlled error injection, auditing both MOS pre-\ndictors and Audio-LLM judges.\nCapability\nOurs\nETE\nITE\nSJ\nSS\nHuman-annotated ground truth\n✓\n~\n~\n✓\n✓\nDimension-level annotation per sample\n✓\n✗\n✗\n✗\n✗\nLinguistically-grounded aspect taxonomy\n✓\n✗\n✗\n✗\n✗\nCovers MOS predictors & Audio-LLM judges\n✓\n✗\n✗\n✓\n✓\nAs deployment scales, however, relying solely on\nhuman judgment becomes expensive and time con-\nsuming. Two main paradigms have emerged to\nautomatically evaluate speech quality at scale: neu-\nral Mean Opinion Score (MOS) predictors (Mittag\net al., 2021; Saeki et al., 2022), trained to output in-\ndividual, holistic quality scores that reflect human\nMOS; and Audio Large Language Model (Audio-\nLLM) Judges (Zhang et al., 2025; Manakul et al.,\n2026), which prompt audio-capable LLMs to rate\nor compare audio samples. A good speech eval-\nuator should respond to all aspects of perceptual\nquality to which humans attend, yet it is not clear\nwhether models of either type do so (i.e whether in-\ndividual output scores align with quality along any\nor all human-perceptible dimensions). For Audio-\nLLMs in particular, it additionally remains an open\nquestion whether they even have the underlying ca-\npability, and/or can be prompted, to attend to each\ndimension.\nTo better understand and identify the aspects of\nspeech to which MOS models and Audio-LLMs ac-\ntually attend, we construct the first meta-evaluation\nbenchmark for automated TTS evaluators that\nhas annotations based on linguistically-grounded,\nhuman-perceptible speech dimensions.\nOur contributions are as follows:\narXiv:2608.09930v1  [cs.SD]  10 Aug 2026\n\n\n===== PAGE 2 =====\nFigure 1: Dataset Construction Pipeline. Sentences from three sources (Harvard Sentences, EmergentTTS-Eval,\nsynthetic text) are fed through three error-generation pathways: LLM-modified plain text, LLM-modified IPA\ntranscriptions, and direct acoustic manipulation. All pathways pass through the TTS model to produce clean (Score\n1) and error-targeted samples. Samples are then annotated by three linguists on all ten dimensions. After linguist\nannotation, majority filtering and downsampling produce a balanced final dataset of 860 samples.\n• Annotation Schema. A linguistically grounded\nevaluation schema decomposing naturalness into\n10 phonologically and phonetically grounded di-\nmensions across word, prosodic, and paralinguis-\ntic levels. This enables future evaluation to be\naligned with human perception of speech quality.\n• Meta-Evaluation Dataset.\nA per-dimension\nannotated dataset of 860 utterances, labeled by\ntrained linguist raters across the 10 dimensions.\n• Dimension-Level Audit. An evaluation of four\nneural MOS models as well as four Audio-\nLLMs judges across four prompting conditions,\nwith and without reference transcripts.\nThis\nreveals uneven phonetic, prosodic and paralin-\nguistic sensitivity, with neither MOS predictors\nnor Audio-LLMs judges reliably detecting er-\nrors across these dimensions under any condition\ntested.\nOur benchmark enables researchers and practition-\ners to attribute TTS system failures to specific in-\nterpretable dimensions rather than opaque holistic\nscores. This provides a diagnostic foundation that\nprior evaluation frameworks have lacked.\n2\nRelated Works\nAutomated TTS Evaluation.\nThe dominant\nparadigm for scalable TTS evaluation has been\nMOS predictors: learning to approximate human\nnaturalness scores from raw waveforms without a\nreference signal. Through the VoiceMOS Chal-\nlenge series (2022–2024) (Huang et al., 2022,\n2024), Semi-supervised Learning (SSL)-based\nMOS predictors (Patton et al., 2016; Reddy et al.,\n2021; Saeki et al., 2022; Baba et al., 2024) have\ndemonstrated strong system-level correlation with\nhuman judgments, approaching the practical ceil-\ning of aggregate naturalness scoring. However,\nthese methods produce holistic quality signals\nwithout grounding them in independently inter-\npretable linguistic attributes, leaving no diagnostic\npath when a system performs poorly. More re-\ncently, researchers have explored leveraging Audio-\nLLMs as richer judges for TTS evaluation, evalu-\nating speaking style (Chiang et al., 2025), speech\nquality (Zhang et al., 2025; Manakul et al., 2026;\nMonjur and Nirjon, 2025), and speaker consis-\ntency (Lee et al., 2026). Yet, LLM-based judges\ninherit the same limitation: scores remain holistic,\nand the perceptual dimensions driving any given\nrating are opaque.\nTTS Evaluation Benchmarks.\nSeveral bench-\nmarks have moved beyond scalar MOS towards\nricher TTS evaluation (Manku et al., 2025; Huang\net al., 2025), yet share a fundamental limitation:\nevaluation dimensions are organized around pre-\ndefined scenario categories rather than a prin-\ncipled speech quality schema.\nEmergentTTS-\nEval (Manku et al., 2025) introduces a model-\nas-a-judge benchmark spanning six pre-defined\nscenario types (emotions, paralinguistics, foreign\nwords, syntactic complexity, complex pronuncia-\ntion, and questions) and demonstrates that Audio-\nLLMs judges can distinguish broad scenario-level\nvariation. InstructTTS-Eval (Huang et al., 2025)\ntakes a complementary direction, centralizing eval-\nuations on expressive and style-following capa-\nbility of TTS systems across pre-defined dimen-\nsions such as speaking rate, pitch, and emotional\nexpressiveness. However, neither provides human-\nannotated ground truth at the attribute level, making\nit impossible to audit whether automated evalua-\n\n\n===== PAGE 3 =====\ntors track the aspects listeners actually perceive.\nWe bridge this gap with a human-annotated dataset\ngrounded in 10 interpretable aspects across three\nlinguistically-motivated quality dimensions, paired\nwith a calibrated annotation framework, enabling\ndirect attribution of TTS system failures to specific\nand interpretable aspects. More information related\nto other benchmarks can be found at Table 1\n3\nBenchmark Design\n3.1\nEvaluation Schema\nCurrent TTS evaluation conflates perceptually dis-\ntinct failure types under a single quality judgment.\nTerms such as “naturalness,” “fluency,” and “clarity”\nare used interchangeably in MOS elicitation, yet\nlisteners explain their ratings by invoking factors\nranging from pronunciation accuracy to affective\ndelivery (Kirkland et al., 2023), aspects that are\nnot only perceptually distinct but defined at dif-\nferent levels of linguistic structure. Drawing on\nCrystal’s distinction between linguistic and paralin-\nguistic features (Crystal, 1969), we organize evalu-\nation around three levels corresponding to where\neach property is linguistically specified: the lexi-\ncon, the utterance, and the speaker. We propose a\nten-dimension schema in which each attribute is\noperationally defined and perceptually separable\n(see Table 5).\n3.1.1\nWord level.\nQuality at the word level is determined by whether\neach word contains the correct sounds and carries\nstress on the correct syllable. We organize this level\naround fixed properties of lexical entries, including\nboth phoneme identity and stress position. Errors at\nthis level (whether phonetic or stress-related) con-\nstitute word identity errors regardless of sentence\ncontext (Jesse et al., 2017).\nPhonetic accuracy: Whether the sounds in each\nword fall within the acceptable range of realization\nfor their lexical targets.\nLexical stress: Whether primary stress falls on\nthe correct syllable of each word, including appro-\npriate vowel reduction in unstressed syllables.\n3.1.2\nProsodic level.\nQuality at the prosodic level is determined by\nwhether suprasegmental properties are appropri-\nate for the syntactic and semantic properties of\nthe utterance. Prosody is a perceptually separa-\nble dimension that listeners isolate from overall\nspeech quality (Gutierrez et al., 2021) and one\nwhere current systems remain measurably below\nnatural speech (Vallés-Pérez et al., 2021).\nWe define the dimensions below based on lin-\nguistic function.\nIntonation: The pitch contour over phrases\nand sentences, encoding utterance type (e.g. ques-\ntion, statement) and discourse structure (e.g. topic\nboundaries, turn-taking signals).\nProsodic stress: Prominence on specific words\nor phrases relative to the rest of the utterance, de-\ntermined by information structure.\nProsodic boundary appropriateness:\nThe\nchunking of the utterance into prosodic phrases\naligned with syntactic structure, realized through\nboundary tones, lengthening, and pause.\nSpeech rate appropriateness: The overall pace\nof delivery, which can fail without any correspond-\ning failure in pitch or prosodic structure.\n3.1.3\nParalinguistic level.\nQuality at the paralinguistic level is determined by\nwhether the speech conveys speaker characteristics\nappropriate to the intended speaker and content. Al-\nthough paralinguistic dimensions share audio prop-\nerties with prosody, such as pitch and timing, they\nconvey information about the speaker rather than\nthe linguistic message. Following Crystal (1969),\nwe distinguish speaker-stable voice quality features\nfrom affective features that modulate in response to\ncontent and context (see Appendix 6.4). Where no\nexternal reference speaker exists, dimensions are\nassessed relative to the system’s own output.\nEmotional appropriateness: The degree to\nwhich the emotional tone matches the affective\ncontent of the utterance.\nExpressiveness: Natural variation in delivery\nstyle and vocal energy, assessed relative to the sys-\ntem itself.\nHuman plausibility: The degree to which vocal\nqualities fall within the range of physically realiz-\nable human production.\nSpeaker identity consistency: Stability of per-\nceived vocal characteristics across the utterance,\nassessed relative to the system itself.\n3.2\nDataset Construction\nOur dataset consists of 860 samples of annotated\nand majority-voted audios. We generated synthetic\nspeech using Cartesia Sonic-3 and introduced con-\ntrolled errors through text modification and acous-\ntic manipulation. Below we describe our source\ncorpora, annotation scale, error generation meth-\nods, and human annotation procedure.\n\n\n===== PAGE 4 =====\nTable 2: Error generation method, sample counts, and inter-annotator agreement per dimension. n is the balanced\nevaluation set after majority-class downsampling to a 50/50 positive/negative split (positives = negatives = n/2 for\nevery dimension). Krippendorff’s α is reported on this set with 95% bootstrap CIs. Total: n = 860.\nLevel\nDimension\nMethod\nText Source\nManipulation\nn\nKrippendorff’s α\nWord\nPhonetic Accuracy\nLLM-altered IPA\nHarvard Sentences\nPhoneme substitution\n110\n0.767 [0.660, 0.852]\nLexical Stress\nLLM-altered IPA\nHarvard Sentences\nStress mark transposition\n90\n0.821 [0.728, 0.907]\nProsodic\nIntonation\nAcoustic manipulation\nEmergentTTS-Eval\nF0 contour manipulation\n80\n0.469 [0.332, 0.611]\nProsodic Stress\nLLM-altered plain text\nHarvard Sentences\nFunction word quoting\n102\n0.658 [0.542, 0.766]\nProsodic Boundary Placement\nLLM-altered plain text\nSynthetically generated\nStructurally targeted sentences\n88\n0.727 [0.604, 0.834]\nSpeech Rate Appropriateness\nAcoustic manipulation\nHarvard Sentences\nDuration scaling\n128\n0.707 [0.605, 0.801]\nParalinguistic\nEmotional Appropriateness\nAPI-enforced emotion\nSynthetically generated\nText–emotion mismatch via Cartesia\ntag\n46\n0.596 [0.400, 0.767]\nExpressiveness\nAcoustic manipulation\nHarvard Sentences\nF0 manipulation, loudness compres-\nsion, vowel shortening\n94\n0.460 [0.332, 0.597]\nSpeaker Identity Consistency\nAcoustic manipulation\nHarvard Sentences\nCross-speaker fading\n66\n0.713 [0.577, 0.836]\nHuman Plausibility\nAcoustic manipulation\nHarvard Sentences\nInserted\naudio\ndistortions\nand\nglitches\n56\n0.710 [0.565, 0.849]\nTotal\n860\nSource corpora.\nTo systematically cover the\nannotation schema dimensions, we selected ut-\nterances from two existing corpora and synthet-\nically generated additional texts where needed\n(Table 2).\nHarvard Sentences (IEEE Subcom-\nmittee on Subjective Measurements, 1969) pro-\nvide 720 phonetically balanced sentences cover-\ning the English phoneme inventory broadly, well-\nsuited to word-level and some prosodic dimensions.\nEmergentTTS-Eval (Manku et al., 2025) provides\nsentences with complex syntactic structures and\nvaried question types (useful for prosodic bound-\nary and intonation dimensions) alongside sentences\nwith explicitly defined affective content, used for\nemotional appropriateness. For dimensions lacking\nappropriate utterances in these sources, we synthet-\nically generated sample texts (Appendix 7.4).\nScale design.\nAll dimensions are rated as binary\n(0/1), where 0 means any error is present and 1\nmeans the audio is perfect, following the annotation\nschema.\nError generation pipeline.\nFor each source sen-\ntence, we generated utterances at the intended qual-\nity level by introducing targeted errors via one of\nthree pathways (see Figure 1):\n• LLM-altered IPA. Used for Phonetic Accuracy\nand Lexical Stress. An LLM (GPT-5) produces\na modified IPA transcription instantiating the in-\ntended error, passed to Cartesia Sonic-3 (Carte-\nsia AI, 2025) for IPA-driven synthesis (e.g.,\nphoneme substitution: seed /sid/ →/sib/; stress\ntransposition: harvest /\"hAr.vIst/ →/hAr.\"vIst/).\n• LLM-altered plain text.\nUsed for Prosodic\nStress and Prosodic Boundary Placement. For\nstress, quotation marks around LLM-selected\nfunction words induce atypical prominence (e.g.,\nType out three lists “of” orders); for bound-\naries, commas split LLM-selected syntactic con-\nstituents (e.g., I dropped my, keys).\n• API-enforced emotion. Used for Emotional Ap-\npropriateness. Sentences with a predetermined\nemotion are synthesized via Cartesia with deliber-\nately mismatched emotion tags (e.g., My beloved\ndog passed away quietly in his sleep tonight. with\ntag euphoric).\n• Acoustic Manipulation. Used for Intonation,\nSpeech Rate, Expressiveness, Speaker Identity\nConsistency, and Human Plausibility.\nPraat-\nbased manipulation (Boersma, 2001) via Parsel-\nmouth (Jadoul et al., 2018) introduces targeted\nperturbations to a clean synthesis baseline: F0\ncontour manipulation for intonation; duration\nscaling for speech rate; combined F0, loudness,\nand vowel duration modification for expressive-\nness; cross-speaker fading for speaker identity;\nand inserted distortions for human plausibility.\nWe treat these as controlled upper-bound cases: a\njudge that cannot detect a deliberately introduced\nperturbation cannot detect subtler naturally oc-\ncurring deviations of the same type.\nThe prompts and details of the acosutic modi-\nfication are provided in Appendix 7.5 and Ap-\npendix 7.2\n3.3\nHuman Annotation\nRaters.\nEach sample was annotated by three pro-\nfessional linguists with experience in analytical\nwork with natural language data. Three raters is the\npractical minimum for stable Krippendorff’s α esti-\nmation; sample counts per dimension are reported\nin Table 2.\nPilot and guideline refinement.\nTo reduce guide-\nline ambiguity (Uma et al., 2022), we conducted a\npilot annotation (n = 50 samples) with all raters\nand iteratively refined the guidelines after collec-\n\n\n===== PAGE 5 =====\ntive review of disagreements.\nProcedure.\nRaters are initially presented with the\naudio only, but have the option to toggle the tran-\nscript. Every sample is rated on all applicable di-\nmensions, not only the dimension it was generated\nto target. This yields cross-dimensional contam-\nination data and allows us to quantify perceptual\nspillover: when a sample was generated to elicit a\nfailure on dimension X, what do raters observe on\ndimension Y? (See Appendix 2)\nInter-rater reliability.\nTable 2 reports Krippen-\ndorff’s α (Krippendorff, 2011) on the construct-\naligned subset (n = 46-128 per dimension), after\nclass balancing, with 95% bootstrap confidence in-\ntervals (1117 resamples, percentile method, seed\n42).\n3.4\nDataset Finalization\nAggregation and downsampling.\nGround truth\nannotations for each sample, for each dimension,\nwere determined by majority vote. Each intended\n(analysis) dimension was downsampled to a bal-\nanced dataset of positive and negative samples, ran-\ndomly sampling from the larger class with prior-\nity for consensus annotations. Final dataset statis-\ntics, including per-dimension sample counts, are\nreported in Table 2.\n4\nExperiments\n4.1\nJudges Evaluated\nWe evaluate two classes of automated judge that\ndiffer in output type and evaluation mechanism.\nMOS predictors.\nWe include UTMOSv2 (Baba\net al., 2024), DNSMOS-Pro (Cumlin et al., 2024),\nNISQA\n(Mittag et al., 2021), and Audiobox-\nAesthetics (Tjandra et al., 2025). Each produces\neither a single continuous scalar per audio or\ndimension-wise scores.\nAudio-LLMs judges.\nWe evaluate a set of open-\nsource and closed audio large language mod-\nels: Gemini 3.5 Flash , Gemini 3 Flash, Qwen3-\nOmni-30B-A3B and Step-Audio-R2-Mini, Audio-\nLLMs judges are prompt-sensitive, motivating the\nsystematic prompting conditions described in Sec-\ntion 4.2.\n4.2\nPrompting Strategy Conditions\nWe define four prompting conditions varying in\nthe amount of schema guidance provided and the\ngranularity of the expected output. In all conditions,\nmodels produce binary labels matching the ground-\ntruth annotation format. Each judge is queried three\ntimes per sample per condition, with the majority\nlabel taken as the final prediction. All conditions\nare run with and without the reference transcript.\nCondition 1: Underspecified MOS-style prompt.\nThe model is prompted to rate the \"naturalness\" of\nthe sample without a detailed schema, producing\na single holistic score analogous to a neural MOS\npredictor. This condition establishes what the judge\nattends to in the absence of detailed task guidance,\nand serves as the primary baseline.\nCondition 2a: Schema-guided prompt, single\nscore.\nThe model is prompted with the full 10-\ndimension schema but instructed to produce a sin-\ngle overall score. Since this yields one score per\nsample, we correlate it against human labels inde-\npendently for each dimension, treating the same\npredicted score as the candidate signal for each.\nThis condition reveals whether schema exposure\nalone is sufficient to direct the judge toward the\nright perceptual dimensions.\nCondition 2b: Schema-guided prompt, per-di-\nmension scores.\nThe model is prompted with the\nfull schema and instructed to produce a score for\neach dimension, enabling direct per-dimension cor-\nrelation against ground-truth labels. This condition\nestablishes whether models can score all dimen-\nsions simultaneously when given the a detailed\nschema.\nCondition 3: Isolated per-dimension prompts.\nThe model is prompted for each dimension indi-\nvidually. This condition reveals which dimensions\nthe judge can reliably detect without interference\nfrom the full schema. It tests dimension-specific\nsensitivity in its purest form.\nFull prompt text for all conditions is provided in\nAppendix 6.7.\n4.3\nMetrics\nFor each (rating dimension × model) pair, we eval-\nuate how well model scores track binary human\nground-truth labels.\nPer-dimension effect size and significance.\nKendall’s τb serves as a unified comparison metric\nfor per-dimension effect sizes across both model\ntypes: for continuous models (MOS predictors)\nit measures rank-order correlation against binary\nlabels, and for binary models (Audio-LLMs) it is\nalgebraically equivalent to Pearson’s ϕ. This equiv-\nalence enables consistent comparison of continu-\nous and binary models within the same evaluation\nframework.\n\n\n===== PAGE 6 =====\nTable 3: Per-dimension Kendall’s τ against human ground truth per evaluation dimension. AUDIO-LLMS judge\nrows use the C1 naturalness prompt (single holistic score, closest MOS analogue).\nModel\nWord\nProsodic\nParalinguistic\nPhon.\nLex.\nInton.\nPros.S\nPros.B\nRate\nEmo.\nExpr.\nSpk.\nHum.\nMOS Models\nAudioBoxCE\n-0.051\n0.161\n0.371∗∗∗-0.241∗∗\n-0.036\n0.550∗∗∗\n0.050\n0.187∗\n-0.270∗∗\n0.606∗∗∗\nAudioBoxCU\n-0.052\n0.015\n0.098\n0.068\n0.048\n0.485∗∗∗-0.025\n0.418∗∗∗-0.177\n0.511∗∗∗\nAudioBoxPC\n-0.058\n-0.019\n-0.099\n0.337∗∗∗\n0.152\n0.020\n-0.050\n0.326∗∗∗-0.142\n-0.615∗∗∗\nAudioBoxPQ\n-0.088\n0.038\n0.272∗∗\n-0.169∗\n-0.079\n0.447∗∗∗\n0.015\n0.304∗∗∗-0.379∗∗∗\n0.606∗∗∗\nDNSMOS-ProBVCC -0.112\n-0.055\n0.092\n0.121\n0.179\n-0.147∗\n-0.061 -0.072\n-0.196\n0.450∗∗∗\nDNSMOS-ProVCC\n0.005\n-0.121\n0.026\n-0.048\n-0.038\n0.071\n0.128\n0.406∗∗∗-0.092\n0.397∗∗∗\nNISQA\n0.027\n-0.047\n0.068\n0.142\n0.099\n0.236∗∗\n-0.015\n0.347∗∗∗-0.036\n0.377∗∗∗\nUTMOSv2\n0.000\n0.087\n0.289∗∗\n0.200∗\n-0.024\n0.390∗∗∗-0.062\n0.099\n0.050\n0.313∗∗\nAudioLLM Models\nGemini 3 Flash†\n0.323∗∗∗\n0.251∗\n0.125\n0.125\n-0.081\n0.123\n0.089\n0.274∗∗\n0.036\n0.202\nGemini 3 Flash△\n-0.022\n0.089\n-0.225∗\n0.099\n-0.104\n0.091\n0.000 -0.050\n0.036\n0.121\nGemini 3.5 Flash†\n0.412∗∗∗\n0.312∗∗\n0.083\n0.227∗\n0.276∗\n0.116\n0.255\n0.170\n0.254∗\n0.238\nGemini 3.5 Flash△\n-0.091\n0.082\n-0.026\n0.111\n0.230\n0.000\n0.045\n0.135\n0.068\n0.219\nQwen3 Omni†\n0.000\n-0.124\n-0.113\n0.039\n0.064\n0.214∗\n0.105\n0.061\n0.293∗\n0.000\nQwen3 Omni△\n0.173\n0.192\n0.026\n-0.103\n0.262\n0.300∗∗∗\n0.088\n0.138\n0.228\n0.237\nStep Audio 2 Mini†\n-0.018\n0.022\n-0.128\n-0.080\n0.116\n-0.096\n-0.132\n0.065\n0.129\n0.073\nStep Audio 2 Mini△\n0.019\n0.023\n0.100\n0.040\n-0.111\n-0.078\n-0.039 -0.021\n0.095\n0.036\nModel Subscripts:: CE = Content Enjoyment; CU = Content Usefulness; PC = Production Complexity; PQ = Production Quality.\nDNSMOS-Pro subscripts: BVCC = trained on BVCC corpus; VCC = trained on VCC2018 corpus. AUDIOLLM rows use the C1\nnaturalness prompt. Columns: Phon. = Phonetic Accuracy; Lex. = Lexical Stress; Inton. = Intonation; Pros.S = Prosodic Stress;\nPros.B = Prosodic Boundary; Rate = Speech Rate; Emo. = Emotional Appropriateness; Expr. = Expressiveness; Spk. = Speaker\nIdentity; Hum. = Human Plausibility.ç Transcript: † = with transcript; △= without transcript. Bold = significant (p < .05).\n∗p < .05, ∗∗p < .01, ∗∗∗p < .001 (two-sided).\nTo test significance, for MOS predictors (contin-\nuous output), we report Mann-Whitney U, which\ntests for distributional separation between score\ndistributions of positive and negative labels. For\nAudio-LLMs(binary output), we also report Mc-\nNemar’s test, which tests for marginal symmetry\nbetween model predictions and ground truth.\nFor MOS predictors, we additionally report AU-\nROC, which is more naturally interpretable for con-\ntinuous output and is equivalent to a normalized\nMann-Whitney U statistic. AUROC rankings are\nconsistent with effect sizes reported by Kendall’s\nτb across predictors (Spearman’s ρ = 0.995).\n4.4\nMain Findings\nMOS Predictors and AudioLLM Judges show\nsensitivity to different dimensions.\nUnder a ba-\nsic naturalness prompt (C1), MOS predictors and\nAudio-LLMs judges attend to fundamentally differ-\nent aspects of speech quality. Neither class aligns\nreliably with human judgments across the full set\nof perceptual dimensions. We observe that quality\ndegradations in many dimensions go unnoticed by\nthese models.\nEvery MOS predictor reaches significance on\nat least one dimension, with their attention con-\ncentrating on paralinguistic dimensions generated\nvia acoustic manipulation, while remaining insen-\nsitive to word-level and prosodic dimensions (see\nTable 3). Human Plausibility, constructed via in-\nserted glitches, is the most consistently detected\ndimension, reaching significance across all tested\nmodels. Speech Rate Appropriateness, constructed\nvia duration scaling, is also significant across 6 of\nthe 8 models. Both patterns are consistent with\nMOS predictors tracking signal-level degradation,\nreflecting their origins in telephony-era voice qual-\nity assessment where such artifacts were the pri-\nmary target. Expressiveness also reaches signifi-\ncance for six of the eight models, though whether\nthis reflects genuine sensitivity to expressive de-\nlivery or a response to shared low-level acoustic\nproperties across the Expressiveness, Speech Rate,\nand Human Plausibility stimuli remains unclear.\nIn contrast, no MOS models show significant de-\ntection of Phonetic Accuracy, Lexical Stress, and\nProsodic Boundary Placement, dimensions where\nTTS systems produce linguistically meaningful er-\nrors that human listeners reliably detect, but MOS\npredictors do not.\nSome significant correlations are negative.\nWe\nobserve a misalignment between some predictors\nand human perceptual judgments. AudioBoxPC\nshows a strong negative relationship with Hu-\nman Plausibility; negative effects also appear\nacross AudioBoxCE, AudioBoxPQ and DNS-\nMOS ProBVCC on Speaker Identity Consistency,\n\n\n===== PAGE 7 =====\nTable 4: Per-dimension Kendall’s τ for AUDIO-LLMS judges using C2a, C2b, and C3 prompts (with and without\na provided schema, single or per-dimension outputs). Undefined τ (model predicted same value for all samples)\nindicated by (.)\nModel\nWord\nProsodic\nParalinguistic\nPhon.\nLex.\nInton. Pros.S\nPros.B\nRate\nEmo.\nExpr.\nSpk.\nHum.\nC2a — Multi-dimension schema; single holistic score\nGemini 3 Flash†\n0.292∗∗\n0.037\n0.197\n0.141\n0.064\n0.000\n0.264\n0.142\n0.177\n0.135\nGemini 3 Flash△\n0.206∗\n0.327∗∗\n-0.160\n0.000\n0.283∗\n0.172\n0.129\n0.211∗\n0.124\n0.000\nGemini 3.5 Flash†\n0.486∗∗∗\n0.294∗∗\n0.119\n0.216∗\n0.064\n0.000\n0.396∗∗-0.031\n0.122\n0.146\nGemini 3.5 Flash△\n0.396∗∗∗\n0.438∗∗∗-0.052\n0.064\n-0.104\n0.102\n0.099\n0.170\n0.296∗\n0.115\nQwen3 Omni†\n0.097\n0.146\n0.052 -0.101\n0.192\n.\n.\n0.182\n0.435∗∗∗\n0.000\nQwen3 Omni△\n0.194∗\n0.212∗\n.\n-0.141\n0.000\n.\n.\n.\n-0.124\n0.135\nStep Audio 2 Mini†\n0.000\n0.186\n.\n-0.101\n0.000\n0.000\n0.213\n-0.147\n0.413∗∗∗\n0.135\nStep Audio 2 Mini△\n0.041\n0.000\n0.160 -0.039\n-0.111\n-0.221∗\n0.000\n-0.165\n0.061\n-0.153\nC2b — Multi-dimension schema; one score per dimension\nGemini 3 Flash†\n0.397∗∗∗\n0.106\n.\n.\n0.196\n.\n0.149\n0.104\n.\n.\nGemini 3 Flash△\n0.194∗\n0.186\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n0.104\n.\n.\nGemini 3.5 Flash†\n0.514∗∗∗\n0.290∗∗\n.\n.\n0.137\n0.126\n0.309∗\n0.211∗\n0.177\n0.135\nGemini 3.5 Flash△\n0.210∗\n0.106\n0.113 .\n0.137\n0.089\n0.149\n0.147\n0.218\n0.135\nQwen3 Omni†\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\nQwen3 Omni△\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n-0.104\n.\n.\nStep Audio 2 Mini†\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\nStep Audio 2 Mini△.\n.\n0.095 .\n.\n.\n0.149\n.\n.\n.\nC3 — Single-dimension focus; one dimension at a time\nGemini 3 Flash†\n0.366∗∗∗\n0.000\n0.083 .\n0.319∗\n0.089\n0.213\n0.061\n.\n.\nGemini 3 Flash△\n0.261∗∗\n0.267∗\n0.000 .\n0.055\n0.021\n0.213\n-0.054\n.\n0.135\nGemini 3.5 Flash†\n0.355∗∗∗\n0.178\n.\n.\n0.243\n0.181∗\n0.459∗∗-0.060\n0.286∗\n0.135\nGemini 3.5 Flash△\n0.194∗\n0.170\n-0.155\n0.100\n0.276∗\n0.232∗∗\n0.303∗\n0.043\n0.218\n-0.135\nQwen3 Omni†\n.\n.\n.\n.\n0.137\n-0.184∗\n.\n0.026\n.\n0.165\nQwen3 Omni△\n0.136\n0.243∗\n.\n.\n0.298∗\n0.050\n-0.149\n-0.214∗.\n0.139\nStep Audio 2 Mini†\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n0.104\n.\n0.000\nStep Audio 2 Mini△.\n-0.108\n.\n0.100\n.\n.\n.\n0.069\n.\n0.038\nColumns: Phon. = Phonetic Accuracy; Lex. = Lexical Stress; Inton. = Intonation; Pros.S = Prosodic Stress; Pros.B = Prosodic\nBoundary;\nRate = Speech Rate;\nEmo. = Emotional Appropriateness;\nExpr. = Expressiveness;\nSpk. = Speaker Identity;\nHum. = Human Plausibility. Transcript: † = with transcript; △= without transcript. Bold = significant (p < .05). ∗p < .05,\n∗∗p < .01, ∗∗∗p < .001 (two-sided).\nProsodic Stress and Speech Rate. We hypothesize\nthat these predictors assign higher scores to the\ncontrolled acoustic distortions applied to the par-\nalinguistic dimensions (see Appendix 7.3), rating\ndegraded samples more favorably than human lis-\nteners do.\nGeneric naturalness prompts yield sparse, in-\nconsistent sensitivity.\nIn contrast to MOS mod-\nels, Audio-LLMs judges prompted for a generic\nnaturalness rating (C1) show sparse and scattered\nattention to human-perceptible dimensions (see Ta-\nble 3). Gemini 3.5 Flash with transcript shows the\nbroadest coverage of any Audio-LLMs condition,\nreaching significance on dimensions spanning the\nword-level, prosodic, and paralinguistic tiers, while\nevery other model and condition reaches at most\ntwo or three significant dimensions. Beyond the\nGemini family, sensitivity under C1 is sparse to\nabsent, with no dimension reliably detected across\nmore than one model, and the few significant ef-\nfects are directionally inconsistent. Taken together,\nthe C1 baseline shows selective sensitivity across\nthe Gemini models, while the other models show\nlittle evidence of reliable sensitivity to any dimen-\nsion under an underspecified naturalness prompt.\nSchema guidance helps selectively.\nWe find that\nschema guidance recovers word-level sensitivity\nabsent under the unguided naturalness prompt (C1):\nproviding the full 10-dimension schema while\nmaintaining a single aggregated score (C2a) in-\ncreases sensitivity to word-level dimensions for\nmodels that showed none under C1, suggesting that\nthis capacity was present but not elicited without\nschema guidance (see Table 4).\nThe clearest gain appears in the no-transcript\ncondition: both Gemini variants show no signifi-\n\n\n===== PAGE 8 =====\ncant word-level correlations under the basic natu-\nralness prompt (C1) but achieve statistically sig-\nnificant correlations when provided the detailed\nschema (C2a). This supports schema guidance\nimproving model sensitivity on word-level dimen-\nsions, and underscores how under-specified a bare\nnaturalness prompt is for surfacing sensitivity to\nwhich the model evidently has access.\nHowever, this benefit does not extend uniformly\nacross all dimensions: some correlations signifi-\ncant under C1 lose significance under C2a, while\nnew ones emerge. Paralinguistic dimensions re-\nmain largely resistant across all conditions, with\nsignificant correlations sparse and inconsistent.\nOutput collapse (where a model predicts the\nsame score for every sample, leaving correlation\nundefined) is also observed under C2a; for exam-\nple, Step Audio 2 Mini assigns the maximum score\nto every sample regardless of true label.\nWhen models are provided the schema and also\nrequired to score each dimension independently\n(C2b), output collapse becomes the dominant fail-\nure mode. Two of the four models assign the maxi-\nmum score across nearly all dimensions and both\ntranscript conditions, leaving correlation undefined\nin almost all cells.\nTranscript access does not consistently increase\nsensitivity to any dimension.\nTranscript avail-\nability produces inconsistent effects across condi-\ntions and models, with no clean directional pattern.\nThe models that show sensitivity to word-level di-\nmensions under the unguided naturalness prompt\nC1 (Gemini 3 Flash and Gemini 3.5 Flash) only do\nso when the transcript is provided. However, once\nthe schema is provided (C2a), this dependency does\nnot persist; both Gemini variants become signifi-\ncant on the two word-level dimensions without the\ntranscript, although transcript provision is associ-\nated with stronger effect sizes.\nWhen models are prompted for each dimension\nindependently (C3), both Gemini variants remain\nsignificant on Phonetic Accuracy with stronger ef-\nfect sizes when the transcript is provided. For\nparalinguistic dimensions, Gemini 3.5 Flash also\nreaches significance on Emotional Appropriateness\nin both transcript conditions. However, the pattern\nruns the opposite way for paralinguistic dimensions\nfor two other models; Qwen3 Omni and Step Audio\n2 Mini show significant Speaker Identity sensitivity\nunder C2a only with the transcript, and show no\nsensitivity to the other paralinguistic dimensions in\neither condition.\nThe absence of a consistent transcript benefit\nsuggests that current Audio-LLMs judges are not\nconsistent in their ability to leverage textual ground-\ning to improve dimension-specific detection, even\nfor dimensions such as Phonetic Accuracy where\nthe reference transcript is directly informative.\nAudio-LLMs sensitivity remains inconsistent un-\nder per-dimension prompting.\nUnder C3 (per-\ndimension prompting), sensitivity remains sparse\nand inconsistent, with a pattern that largely dif-\nfers from C1 and C2a. An exception is Gemini’s\nincreased sensitivity to Phonetic Accuracy under\ntranscript provision, which holds across prompt\nvariations. Negative correlations still exist for some\nmodels on isolated dimensions. Step Audio 2 Mini\nshows no significant correlation under any tran-\nscript condition in C3, consistent with its perfor-\nmance across all other conditions and suggesting\na fundamental sensitivity limitation rather than a\nprompting artifact.\n5\nConclusion\nWe\npresent\nthe\nfirst\ndimension-level\nmeta-\nevaluation benchmark for TTS, decomposing natu-\nralness into 10 linguistically grounded perceptual\nattributes annotated by trained linguists. Across\nfour MOS predictors and four Audio-LLMs judges,\nwe find that current automated evaluators ex-\nhibit systematic but distinct blind spots: MOS\npredictors align strongly with signal-level arti-\nfacts but fail on word-level and prosodic dimen-\nsions, whereas Audio-LLMs judges show selective,\nprompt-dependent detection that never generalises\nacross the full dimension set.\nWe further show that how Audio-LLMs judges\nare prompted in practice substantially affects their\nmeasured performance: querying dimensions in\nisolation consistently improves alignment with hu-\nman judgments, while asking models to score\nall dimensions jointly degrades output reliability\nthrough prediction collapse.\nTaken together, our findings suggest that natural-\nness cannot be treated as a single scalar construct\nfor modern TTS evaluation and that robust evalua-\ntion will require dimension-aware benchmarks.\nLimitations\nEcological validity. Due to the difficulty of sourc-\ning naturally occurring TTS failures at scale across\nspecific perceptual dimensions, errors were elicited\nthrough TTS prompting and Praat manipulation.\n\n\n===== PAGE 9 =====\nPraat-manipulated stimuli are thus best understood\nas controlled upper-bound conditions rather than\nrepresentative deployment samples.\nStimulus complexity ceiling. Harvard Sentences\naverage 7–8 words with SVO structure, limited dis-\ncourse context, and few idioms or proper nouns.\nThis is ideal for eliciting clear word-level failures,\nbut limits generalisability to more complex natural-\nistic speech where prosodic and syntactic interac-\ntions are richer.\nSingle architecture for IPA-controlled genera-\ntion. Phoneme-level input is currently only sup-\nported by Cartesia Sonic among the evaluated\nproviders; all IPA-driven samples consequently\nreflect one TTS architecture. We identify multi-\nsystem evaluation as a priority for future work.\nAnnotation scale granularity. Binary scales\ndo not capture full fine-grained quality gradients.\nIn contrast, ordinal scales provide granularity but\nalso the potential for response biases and incon-\nsistent boundary interpretation between levels. In\nthis context, binary scales were a deliberate scope\nchoice; the benchmark targets error detection, not\nfull perceptual grading.\nAnnotation reliability. Majority-class downsam-\npling to achieve class balance reduces effective\nsample sizes, most consequentially for Emotional\nAppropriateness (n=46) and Lexical Stress (n=90),\nwhere agreement estimates carry wider confidence\nintervals.\nEthics Statement\nOur proposed benchmark is intended solely to sup-\nport more targeted and interpretable TTS evalua-\ntion research and is not intended for discrimina-\ntory applications or commercial ranking of TTS\nproviders. All annotators are first-language English\nspeakers and trained linguists who participated vol-\nuntarily at standard rate compensation. No sensi-\ntive or personally identifiable data was collected.\nAll of our speech samples are synthetically gen-\nerated or acoustically manipulated; no real human\nspeech recordings were collected. Source corpora\nand APIs are used in accordance with their re-\nspective licenses and terms of service (see Ap-\npendix 7.5).\nRegarding Large Language Model (LLM) usage\nin manuscript preparation, we utilize them solely to\nrefine the language used in paper to improve clarity\nand correctness, without generating any substantial\ncontent or claims.\nReferences\nKaito Baba, Wataru Nakata, Yuki Saito, and Hiroshi\nSaruwatari. 2024. The t05 system for the VoiceMOS\nChallenge 2024: Transfer learning from deep im-\nage classifier to naturalness 