{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b4cca0adcecbadfc", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# SpeechJudge: Towards Human-Level Judgment for Speech Naturalness\narXiv: 2511.07931\nAuthors: Xueyao Zhang, Chaoren Wang, Huan Liao, Ziniu Li, Yuancheng Wang, Li Wang, Dongya Jia, Yuanzhe Chen, Xiulin Li, Zhuo Chen, Zhizheng Wu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: audio_understanding\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of SpeechJudge-Data, a large-scale 99K-pair human feedback dataset for speech naturalness and intelligibility constructed from diverse zero-shot TTS models, speech styles, and languages.\n  2. Establishment of SpeechJudge-Eval, a challenging benchmark for speech naturalness judgment that reveals significant performance gaps in existing metrics and AudioLLMs.\n  3. Development of SpeechJudge-GRM, a generative reward model trained via a two-stage post-training process of supervised fine-tuning with Chain-of-Thought data and reinforcement learning on challenging cases.\n  4. Demonstration that SpeechJudge-GRM outperforms classic Bradley-Terry reward models on the SpeechJudge-Eval benchmark while supporting inference-time scaling and explainability.\n  5. Verification that SpeechJudge-GRM serves as an effective reward function for high-quality sample selection and post-training alignment of speech generation models with human preferences.\n\nTags: Eval, Eval/LLM, LALM, LALM/后训练-GRPO\n===\n# EmergentTTS-Eval: Evaluating TTS Models on Complex Prosodic, Expressiveness, and Linguistic Challenges Using Model-as-a-Judge\narXiv: 2505.23009\nAuthors: Ruskin Raj Manku, Yuzhi Tang, Xingjian Shi, Mu Li, Alex Smola\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_dataset\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We create a benchmark with 1,645 samples for evaluating TTS systems across six challenging scenarios: Emotions, Paralinguistics, Syntactic Complexity, Questions, Foreign Words and Complex Pronunciation.\n  2. We propose an iterative refinement strategy with LLMs that creates increasingly complex utterances for TTS, resulting in a multi-layered and diverse evaluation benchmark.\n  3. We are the first to use Large Audio Language Models as reward models for judging subjective dimensions of audio, demonstrating its effectiveness through high correlation with human preferences and stability across different judger models.\n  4. We evaluate leading open-source and closed-source TTS systems on our benchmark, revealing fine-grained systematic failures and highlighting performance gaps between closed-source and open-source models.\n\nTags: Eval/基准, Eval/LLM, Eval/听觉感知", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "356455f0-e3c6-4e87-9d48-a787cab68dc3", "uuid": "285ad460-fc11-4d4b-8dfe-53ff344399c7", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T08:37:52.435Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# SpeechJudge: Towards Human-Level Judgment for Speech Naturalness\narXiv: 2511.07931\nAuthors: Xueyao Zhang, Chaoren Wang, Huan Liao, Ziniu Li, Yuancheng Wang, Li Wang, Dongya Jia, Yuanzhe Chen, Xiulin Li, Zhuo Chen, Zhizheng Wu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: audio_understanding\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of SpeechJudge-Data, a large-scale 99K-pair human feedback dataset for speech naturalness and intelligibility constructed from diverse zero-shot TTS models, speech styles, and languages.\n  2. Establishment of SpeechJudge-Eval, a challenging benchmark for speech naturalness judgment that reveals significant performance gaps in existing metrics and AudioLLMs.\n  3. Development of SpeechJudge-GRM, a generative reward model trained via a two-stage post-training process of supervised fine-tuning with Chain-of-Thought data and reinforcement learning on challenging cases.\n  4. Demonstration that SpeechJudge-GRM outperforms classic Bradley-Terry reward models on the SpeechJudge-Eval benchmark while supporting inference-time scaling and explainability.\n  5. Verification that SpeechJudge-GRM serves as an effective reward function for high-quality sample selection and post-training alignment of speech generation models with human preferences.\n\nTags: Eval, Eval/LLM, LALM, LALM/后训练-GRPO\n===\n# EmergentTTS-Eval: Evaluating TTS Models on Complex Prosodic, Expressiveness, and Linguistic Challenges Using Model-as-a-Judge\narXiv: 2505.23009\nAuthors: Ruskin Raj Manku, Yuzhi Tang, Xingjian Shi, Mu Li, Alex Smola\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_dataset\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We create a benchmark with 1,645 samples for evaluating TTS systems across six challenging scenarios: Emotions, Paralinguistics, Syntactic Complexity, Questions, Foreign Words and Complex Pronunciation.\n  2. We propose an iterative refinement strategy with LLMs that creates increasingly complex utterances for TTS, resulting in a multi-layered and diverse evaluation benchmark.\n  3. We are the first to use Large Audio Language Models as reward models for judging subjective dimensions of audio, demonstrating its effectiveness through high correlation with human preferences and stability across different judger models.\n  4. We evaluate leading open-source and closed-source TTS systems on our benchmark, revealing fine-grained systematic failures and highlighting performance gaps between closed-source and open-source models.\n\nTags: Eval/基准, Eval/LLM, Eval/听觉感知", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
